WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.486
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:13.980
JANE GOULD: You
patient souls who've

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.120
been here for a long time
holding your seats, welcome.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.540
I'm Jane Gould, the coordinator
of the Technology and Culture

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:22.620
Forum.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.220
And I'd like to welcome
you to tonight's program,

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.860
"Noam Chomsky on the
Foundations of World Order--

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:34.890
50 Years of the UN, the World
Bank, the IMF, the Universal

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.680
Declaration of Human Rights."

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.810
Before we turn to
tonight's program,

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:43.830
I call your attention to the
Technology and Culture Forum's

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:47.310
next program,
"Reinventing Universities

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.580
for the 21st Century."

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:53.710
There is a sign with all the
information above my head.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:55.620
And do come.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:00.960
Wednesday, March 3,
at 5:30 PM in 6120.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.590
The Technology and Culture
Forum will be hosting four more

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.570
programs this spring.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.120
If you'd like to be
on our mailing list,

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.730
you can either sign on
one of the sheets of paper

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:16.470
on the table in the hall as
you leave, or go to our website

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:20.760
and just put yourself
onto the list.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.150
Now to tonight's program.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:27.240
We're all here because we
want to hear Noam Chomsky.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.690
Chomsky is honored by MIT
as an institute professor.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:38.650
His doctoral thesis on
transformational analysis

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:43.270
began his radical transformation
of the field of linguistics.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:47.320
Honorary degrees, learned
in professional societies,

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.660
significant awards--

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:52.060
his list is luminous.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:56.380
Few have escaped his notice.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.540
And yet, as a
public intellectual,

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:02.110
Professor Chomsky has
always taken seriously

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:07.630
his responsibility to stimulate
and lead public debate.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.370
In addition to
speaking and writing

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.920
on linguistics and
philosophy, he's

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.200
taken on intellectual
history, contemporary issues,

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.770
international affairs,
US foreign policy,

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:24.470
to name a few of his key topics.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:27.670
Late this last year,
I received an email

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:32.230
encouraging me to participate
in a 70th birthday

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.200
card for Noam Chomsky.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.210
Online, global--
hundreds, thousands--

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:42.130
the list went on,
and people couldn't

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.880
resist saying how they'd
been challenged and inspired

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.650
by Chomsky.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:52.180
Tonight, he's here to
look at the last 50 years,

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:57.910
what we created, what we
have, and where we are to go.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:58.840
Noam Chomsky.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:02.270
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:18.800
NOAM CHOMSKY: I just realized
while listening to Jane

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.470
announce the title
that it's also

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:24.470
just about 50 years since
I walked into this building

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.220
for the first time.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:29.210
But I won't talk about that.

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:35.210
We've just passed the 50th
anniversary of the Universal

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:37.550
Declaration of Human Rights.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.620
That was a few weeks ago.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.800
The Human Rights
Regime, which was

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.760
encoded in that
declaration, is one

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.620
of the pillars of the
system of world order

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:56.380
that was constructed on the
wreckage of World War II.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:02.780
There were two other
major foundation stones.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:06.560
One was the International
Political Order,

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.980
which is articulated in
the United Nations Charter.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:14.690
And the third is the
International Economic System,

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:17.930
sometimes called the Bretton
Woods System, designed

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:22.150
primarily by the United
States and Britain

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.110
right at the end of the war.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:27.950
These three systems
were closely integrated,

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:30.930
conceptually and in fact.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:34.490
The thinking behind
them illustrates

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:39.470
that, as does their
interactions over the years.

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.850
And to a degree, that's quite
unusual in world affairs.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:50.210
The three foundations reflected
public attitudes and concerns

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:53.390
over quite a wide range.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:58.790
For that very reason,
the principles that were

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:03.290
articulated and, to some
extent, instituted--

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:10.190
those principles were quite
distasteful to elite elements,

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:12.260
namely those who were
actually in a position

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:19.550
to construct and shape and
guide the actual world order.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:22.940
And they very quickly
took steps to dismantle,

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:27.290
or at least attenuate,
the lofty principles.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:32.420
The conflicts over these
matters constitute a large part

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:34.130
of modern history--

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.170
post Cold War history.

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:41.390
That's not the usual framework
of analysis for discussing it.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:44.390
But in my opinion,
it ought to be.

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:47.340
Well, large issues.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:48.890
There's a lot in print.

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:54.950
There's many treatises yet to be
written even to be researched.

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:58.130
I don't think the topics have
been addressed with anything

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:00.280
like enough seriousness.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:03.950
But I'll try to give
some indication of why

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:09.230
I think that's an appropriate
and instructive way to view

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:13.315
the contemporary world system.

00:06:13.315 --> 00:06:16.820
It's our origins at the time
of the Second World War.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:19.670
And maybe it's likely future.

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:23.480
So the main question
that I want to get to

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:29.660
is, what has been the fate of
the three basic and integrated

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:34.190
pillars of world order that were
established half a century ago?

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:37.820
And specifically, what has been
the role of the United States,

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:41.690
which has been the primary actor
on the world scene throughout--

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:43.050
remains so--

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:45.980
and the one that's
most important for us,

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:48.350
for obvious reasons,
independently

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:53.090
of the significance and
scale of its contributions,

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:57.140
which are usually quite great
for equally obvious reasons.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:00.050
Well, that's the main question
that I want to get to.

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:04.370
But I'd like to
approach it by a detour

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:08.180
just to make life
more complicated.

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:10.970
And the detour has
two tracks that I'd

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:14.180
like to explore a little
bit, and then, from them,

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:16.380
get back to the questions.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:22.160
The first track is
simply to remind everyone

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.390
of what you already know.

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:28.020
We have to bear in mind that
the questions are not abstract

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:31.460
and they're not about
some distant planet.

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:35.930
So it's not like an academic
topic for an academic seminar.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:39.110
We're dealing with
questions of life and death,

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:43.790
of suffering and
pain and despair.

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:46.790
The voices that are heard--

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:47.370
not that one.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:47.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:52.520
The voices that
are heard [LAUGHS]

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:56.720
are those of the rich and
the powerful, naturally.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.020
There are also those
who have sought to be

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:02.120
a voice for the voiceless.

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:06.050
Their fate hasn't
been too happy.

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:11.110
Some were simply
assassinated by our hands,

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:15.550
or those working for us--
a chapter of modern history

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:19.330
the one doesn't
read about too much.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:21.890
In fact, they were assassinated.

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:23.860
And there's quite
a number of them,

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:27.610
doubly in that they were first
killed and then silenced.

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:32.169
So you can do a check and
see how many of your friends

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:35.980
can tell you the names of
Eastern European dissidents

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:37.630
and murdered.

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:40.650
There are murdered counterparts
in Central America.

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:43.510
And how many books you've
read by one and by the other

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:44.380
and so on.

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:45.790
It's an instructive lesson.

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:51.400
But the voices that we
hear, the ones that remain,

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:54.130
are typically the powerful.

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:58.000
And that's important because
that's not the only voice.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:01.240
That's the voice of
a small minority here

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:04.840
and a tiny minority worldwide.

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:09.370
Well, let me illustrate
from right now.

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:13.510
There are major stories in
the press these last few days

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:17.170
on the G7 meetings-- the
meetings of the seven richest

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:19.060
industrial countries--

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:24.560
and on the interchanges
between their leaders.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:29.050
So for example, president
Chirac's, of France,

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:33.220
his interchanges with
Robert Rubin, who I guess

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:36.145
might be called co-president
of the United States.

00:09:36.145 --> 00:09:37.880
[SOFT LAUGHTER]

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:41.020
We have to give Alan Greenspan
at least half the presidency.

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:43.821
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:47.830
So that's been all
over the front pages.

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:50.680
And those discussions
are interesting

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:55.390
and tell you a lot if you
look at them closely--

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:57.070
even not so closely.

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:58.720
The outcome of
these discussions--

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:02.840
G7 and the other
interchanges-- reveal the power

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:05.710
of the United States in
a rather dramatic form,

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:09.730
and also its extreme isolation,
even among the richest

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:10.810
countries.

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:12.400
And if you look a
little more closely,

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:16.000
and you'll learn a lot about
the true nature of the actually

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:19.390
functioning international
economic system,

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:23.710
of the difference between
the doctrines that

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:28.930
apply to the rich and the
powerful by their insistence,

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:31.810
and the opposite
doctrines, which

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:34.030
are imposed on everyone else.

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:37.360
That comes out with great
starkness in the articles

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:41.230
discussing these issues.

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:43.510
I'll come back to
that in connection

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:46.120
with the third pillar of
world order, the International

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:47.530
Economic System.

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:50.620
Well, there are no stories--

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:52.570
and I mean none--

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:57.070
on the G15 meetings, which
have taken place at same time

00:10:57.070 --> 00:11:01.435
in Jamaica in the
last couple of weeks.

00:11:01.435 --> 00:11:05.590
In the national press,
it's literally zero.

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:09.640
I rely on a database
search done by a friend who

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:13.000
has access to that monster.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.480
The New York Times,
The Washington Post,

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:20.060
and The Wall Street Journal
have no word on them.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:21.610
There is no shortage
of information.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:23.350
The Associated
Press had stories,

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:24.970
except they weren't run.

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:29.110
And the BBC World Services
had extensive coverage.

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:31.340
And if you look around
the peripheral press,

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:34.250
particularly in Florida,
there was coverage--

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:37.930
Florida, I presume, because of
the Latin American connection.

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:40.330
But the national
press blanked it out.

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:45.340
Now, these aren't
minor countries.

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:46.870
These are major.

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:48.970
These are not what are
dismissed as "basket

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:52.160
cases" like sub-Saharan Africa.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:56.990
This is Argentina, Brazil,
Chile, Mexico, India,

00:11:56.990 --> 00:11:59.680
Indonesia, and Nigeria--

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:02.290
quite substantial
countries in which people

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:05.290
expect to make a lot
of money if they can.

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:08.620
17 of them, even though
it's called G15--

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:10.180
a good part of the world.

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:12.880
And they do have something
to say, believe it or not.

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:14.590
And you can even read it.

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:16.570
For example, you read
it on the front page

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:21.360
if you happen to subscribe to
the leading journal in Egypt.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:23.024
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:23.024 --> 00:12:27.730
--The Al-Ahram, which
has an English edition

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:30.600
so you can read it in English.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:35.440
What they say at the G15 meeting
is that the United States

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:37.750
and Britain-- this
is mostly quotes--

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:41.950
are unwilling to enter into
a dialogue with the South.

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:43.810
"The South" is what
is euphemistically

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:47.470
called the developing world.

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:49.450
Which direction
they're developing,

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:51.550
you can argue about.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:57.730
The US and Britain are unwilling
to enter into a dialogue

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:00.940
with the South, which is always
forced to make concessions

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:04.330
in the World Trade Organization
to the benefit of the rich.

00:13:04.330 --> 00:13:08.020
The true story of
globalization, they continue,

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:10.180
is that the North--

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:13.810
that's G7-- has to
make maximum benefits,

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:16.690
and the South is only
entitled to a limited margin

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:17.990
of development.

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:21.490
And if this margin is crossed,
the Western speculators

00:13:21.490 --> 00:13:25.340
are there to take you down
as quickly as they can.

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:26.230
That's not false.

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:28.460
But it's a considerable
understatement

00:13:28.460 --> 00:13:30.970
than the writers surely know it.

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:34.390
US power and violence
has also been there

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:41.200
to take you down as quickly
as it can if countries tried

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:44.890
to pursue the path of
dependent development, what's

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:51.760
called in US planning
circles "radical nationalism"

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:54.760
or "economic nationalism"
or sometimes even

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:56.470
"excessive development."

00:13:56.470 --> 00:14:00.700
That's not to be permitted
in the current period

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:04.450
of globalization, for
reasons I'll get back to.

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:06.730
You don't have to send
the Marines that often.

00:14:06.730 --> 00:14:11.482
The speculators can do the job.

00:14:11.482 --> 00:14:18.490
The G15 meeting goes on to issue
a plea to Western investors.

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:21.580
It says, we don't
want to stifle you,

00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:23.650
but we want to know who you are.

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:27.460
And we want you to come and
go in an orderly fashion.

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:30.910
That's precisely what the
North will not accept.

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:34.180
The demand that not be
accepted is a core part

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:37.010
of the Multilateral
Agreement on Investment,

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:42.060
which has been deflected
thanks to activist pressure,

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:46.650
which succeeded in escaping
media controls, but deflected--

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:47.760
not stopped--

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.260
coming back in other ways.

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:56.215
And the point is to prevent the
G15 plea from being realized.

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:02.580
This, then, is accompanied
by grim accounts

00:15:02.580 --> 00:15:06.120
of the effects of the
specific form of globalization

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:09.390
that's been instituted
in the past 25 years.

00:15:09.390 --> 00:15:13.660
It has two major aspects
that are relevant here.

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:16.660
One is that it's been
an economic failure,

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:18.840
from the point of view
of statistics at least--

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:21.090
not from the point of
view of rich people.

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:25.960
So economic growth has slowed
over most of the world.

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:32.130
And as a fraction of
gross domestic product,

00:15:32.130 --> 00:15:34.950
as a fraction of
economic product,

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:40.560
the proportion that goes to
working people has declined.

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:43.620
So working people are
getting a declining part

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:46.110
of a slowing economy.

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:49.170
That's essentially the story.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:53.810
The long-term tendency of this
is a form of globalization.

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:56.610
It's kind of a globalization
of the structural model

00:15:56.610 --> 00:15:58.210
of the third world.

00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:00.780
And that's a very
clear structural model.

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:05.305
You find it just about anywhere,
which is a relic of European--

00:16:05.305 --> 00:16:08.920
and I include here,
US-- imperialism.

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:14.730
So you find societies
with a very small sector

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:17.880
of extremely, extremely
wealthy people, usually linked

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:22.980
to the foreign masters, and
a large number of people

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:28.380
who are somewhere between
survival and suffering,

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:31.040
and a category of
superfluous people.

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:33.570
In our dependencies,
they're often

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:37.200
called the "disposable
people," as in Colombia,

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:39.540
where you dispose of
them-- send out the death

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:41.980
squads or the paramilitaries.

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:43.740
We're a more civilized society.

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:51.150
We throw them in jail instead on
various fraudulent crime words.

00:16:51.150 --> 00:16:54.570
But that model is spreading.

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:56.880
It takes a different
form in a rich country,

00:16:56.880 --> 00:17:01.170
like the United States, than a
poor country like, say, Haiti.

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:03.810
But the structure
is quite similar.

00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:06.869
And the United States
and Britain, it's

00:17:06.869 --> 00:17:08.640
pretty, very explicit.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:13.109
And the rest, in the other rich
countries, it's partially true.

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:16.000
And it's devastatingly
true in most of the world,

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:17.829
including now
Eastern Europe, now

00:17:17.829 --> 00:17:21.300
that the end of the Cold
War has led to it's more

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:23.349
or less predictable outcome.

00:17:23.349 --> 00:17:24.990
And the countries
of Eastern Europe

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:29.710
are returning to what they
were before that attempt

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:31.570
at independent nationalism.

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:35.610
So the ones that were deep,
third-world poverty like Russia

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:39.180
are returning to that status.

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:42.030
The ones that were part of the
West, like the Czech Republic,

00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:45.030
are returning to that
status, about as one

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:46.926
should have expected.

00:17:46.926 --> 00:17:48.300
In my opinion,
that's pretty much

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:50.440
what the Cold War was about.

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:55.440
But so that model
is a familiar one.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:57.570
And it's now being
kind of globalized.

00:17:57.570 --> 00:17:59.790
That's the
globalization process.

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:02.880
Well, that's the G15 view of it.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:08.010
You get a much more
pained and anguished view

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:09.700
when you get to the
poorer countries.

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:15.126
Those are the richer countries
outside the really rich world.

00:18:15.126 --> 00:18:17.025
Well, so they are silenced.

00:18:17.025 --> 00:18:18.615
I'll take a second example.

00:18:21.781 --> 00:18:25.980
A year ago, the World
Trade Organization

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:31.150
celebrated the 50th anniversary
of the global trading system.

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:34.970
It was originally GATT, now
the World Trade Organization.

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:37.500
And that one got plenty
of media coverage.

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:43.770
There was lots of coverage of
the inspired rhetoric by Bill

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:47.070
Clinton and world
leaders, talked

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:50.640
about the marvels that had been
achieved through global trade

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:52.110
and so on.

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:55.250
There was also a spokesperson
for the United Nations there.

00:18:55.250 --> 00:18:58.950
It was the Secretary General
of UNCTAD, the UN Conference

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:03.240
on Trade and Development--
the main United Nations

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:06.810
economic research and
analysis organization, which

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:09.450
is, according to its
charter, committed

00:19:09.450 --> 00:19:11.430
to an international
trading system that

00:19:11.430 --> 00:19:14.420
promotes economic and
social development.

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:16.530
And he gave a talk
too, representing

00:19:16.530 --> 00:19:20.600
the rest of the world,
the United Nations,

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:22.860
as an economist.

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:25.440
And he closed his
speech by saying,

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:28.470
"No one should be fooled
by the festive atmosphere

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:30.170
of these celebrations.

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:34.740
Outside there is anguish and
fear, insecurity about jobs,

00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:38.100
and a life of
quiet desperation."

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:41.190
Well, there was ample
media coverage, as I said.

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:44.120
But it much preferred--
in fact, kept to--

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:47.060
the festive atmosphere within.

00:19:47.060 --> 00:19:49.050
Although, again,
you can read this

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:52.880
if you happen to subscribe to
a very good Third World journal

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:55.640
that's published in
Penang, Malaysia.

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:01.640
There's a name for that.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:03.870
It's called free press, I think.

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:04.370
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:04.370 --> 00:20:09.950
The Secretary General of UNCTAD
presented the same message--

00:20:09.950 --> 00:20:13.640
the same message, pretty
much-- at the recent G15

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:16.160
meeting a couple days ago.

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:19.520
He predicted a bleak future
for the vast majority

00:20:19.520 --> 00:20:24.860
of the people of the South,
although a small sector

00:20:24.860 --> 00:20:29.270
is and will continue
to be quite wealthy.

00:20:29.270 --> 00:20:31.670
Well, the story
that he describes

00:20:31.670 --> 00:20:35.780
is also similar here, including
the part about insecurity.

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:38.150
And that's not exactly a secret.

00:20:38.150 --> 00:20:41.330
So Alan Greenspan,
when he was testifying

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:45.920
before the Congress Senate
Banking Committee, taking pride

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:48.470
in the fairy tale
economy, as it's known,

00:20:48.470 --> 00:20:52.850
over which he presided,
attributed it in large measure

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:56.480
to what he called greater
worker insecurity,

00:20:56.480 --> 00:20:59.420
meaning workers are
just intimidated.

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:01.152
And they're
frightened they're not

00:21:01.152 --> 00:21:02.360
going to have a job tomorrow.

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:05.030
And they're afraid ask for
a raise or for benefits

00:21:05.030 --> 00:21:06.080
and so on.

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:07.880
And that contributes
substantially

00:21:07.880 --> 00:21:10.390
to what's called the
health of the economy.

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:13.160
It's a concept
that's uncorrelated

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:15.920
with the health of the
people in the economy,

00:21:15.920 --> 00:21:19.370
but it's a concept.

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:20.110
It's good.

00:21:20.110 --> 00:21:23.180
It keeps inflation
down, keeps profits up--

00:21:23.180 --> 00:21:25.070
all sorts of good things.

00:21:25.070 --> 00:21:26.280
And it's real.

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:28.990
If you look at polls taken,
say, by Business Week,

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:35.600
you find that like around
90% of working people

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:37.530
are insecure about their job--

00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:39.080
some very insecure.

00:21:39.080 --> 00:21:41.480
About 70% are
afraid that if they

00:21:41.480 --> 00:21:45.650
tried to get involved in union
organizing, they'll be fired.

00:21:45.650 --> 00:21:47.930
The reasons for
this intimidation

00:21:47.930 --> 00:21:51.440
are several, some of them
pretty straightforward.

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:54.530
Some of it is just
straight corporate crime,

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:58.940
which is very easy when it's
backed by a criminal state.

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:02.570
The Reagan administration
made it clear

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:05.300
and open to the business
world that it was simply

00:22:05.300 --> 00:22:07.580
not going to enforce the laws.

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:10.580
As a result, the firing
of union organizers

00:22:10.580 --> 00:22:13.430
went way up--
approximately tripled.

00:22:13.430 --> 00:22:17.450
And other measures could be
used to ensure that workers

00:22:17.450 --> 00:22:19.040
would stay intimidated.

00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:22.170
That continues under the
Clinton administration.

00:22:22.170 --> 00:22:26.410
Another measure
of intimidation is

00:22:26.410 --> 00:22:30.260
what are mislabeled
"free trade agreements."

00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:31.520
They're not about free trade.

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:34.100
And they're surely
not agreements--

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:35.872
at least, if the
population matters.

00:22:35.872 --> 00:22:37.580
The fact that they're
called "agreements"

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:40.630
is interesting, because it's
recognized that, at least

00:22:40.630 --> 00:22:42.380
in any country that's
democratic enough

00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:44.850
to take polls like Canada
and the United States,

00:22:44.850 --> 00:22:46.790
that the population
is against them.

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:48.530
But they're agreements,
nevertheless,

00:22:48.530 --> 00:22:52.130
which tells you something about
the conception of democracy.

00:22:52.130 --> 00:22:55.850
If they were about free trade,
say, cutting back tariffs,

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:57.680
NAFTA could have taken 2 pages.

00:22:57.680 --> 00:23:01.349
It's 2,200 pages because
it's not about free trade.

00:23:01.349 --> 00:23:03.140
But what are called
"free trade agreements"

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:07.940
have the property that they are
a way of threatening workers.

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:11.960
So there are even some
studies on it, one

00:23:11.960 --> 00:23:15.690
by a very good labor
historian at Cornell, Kate

00:23:15.690 --> 00:23:19.820
Bronfenbrenner, showing
that in about half

00:23:19.820 --> 00:23:22.280
of organizing
efforts since NAFTA,

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:27.380
there's been an effort to
disrupt by threat of transfer.

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:29.090
And it's not an unreal threat.

00:23:29.090 --> 00:23:31.490
Where organizing has
nevertheless succeeded,

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:34.460
the number of
transfers has tripled.

00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:36.080
And if you look
where it's tripled,

00:23:36.080 --> 00:23:38.650
it's in the mobile industries,
like not construction,

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:40.400
but manufacturing.

00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:44.070
So it's a real threat, and
it helps intimidate people.

00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:46.250
And it contributes to the
health of the economy.

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:47.750
And of course, in
a poorer country,

00:23:47.750 --> 00:23:50.430
it's a much greater threat.

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:52.850
So that's part of the
fairy tale economy,

00:23:52.850 --> 00:23:55.650
which is, indeed, a fairy
tale economy for some people,

00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:59.660
including people in the sector
that most of us come from--

00:23:59.660 --> 00:24:00.920
me, in particular.

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:04.400
For that sector of the
population, it's a fairy tale.

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:06.890
When you look at the reports
of the fairy tale economy,

00:24:06.890 --> 00:24:11.340
you find two things mentioned--
return on capital and the stock

00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:12.350
market.

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:15.890
And for those who
have stock, say

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:19.230
the rapid asset inflation
of the past years

00:24:19.230 --> 00:24:21.530
has made them very rich.

00:24:21.530 --> 00:24:23.490
What it means for the
economy is debated.

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:25.190
But it certainly
made them very rich.

00:24:25.190 --> 00:24:28.340
So it's a fairy tale for
the 1% of the economy who

00:24:28.340 --> 00:24:30.440
own about half the stock--

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:32.690
similar figures
for other assets.

00:24:32.690 --> 00:24:36.860
And for the 10% who own
most of the rest, about 85%

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:40.800
of the benefits have gone to
about 10% of the population.

00:24:40.800 --> 00:24:43.890
If you go to the next
10%, the second decile,

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:48.270
they've actually lost net worth
during the Clinton recovery.

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:52.410
You go down further, it looks
uglier over the past years.

00:24:52.410 --> 00:24:55.920
In fact, this recovery
is unusual and, I think,

00:24:55.920 --> 00:24:58.515
unprecedented.

00:24:58.515 --> 00:25:02.010
First of all, it's
quite slow, even

00:25:02.010 --> 00:25:04.980
by the standards of the
postwar period, certainly--

00:25:04.980 --> 00:25:07.320
not very different from
the sluggish recoveries

00:25:07.320 --> 00:25:09.060
of the '70s and '80s--

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:10.770
much below the earlier ones.

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:13.050
But it's also unusual in
that most of the population

00:25:13.050 --> 00:25:14.580
has been left out.

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:20.700
Just now, after the peak of the
business cycle, it was 1989--

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:22.740
so it's almost 10 years--

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:29.400
just now, the median is getting
back to where it was in 1989.

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:32.715
There's never been such a slow
recovery for the majority--

00:25:32.715 --> 00:25:35.790
it's probably 70%,
80% of the population.

00:25:35.790 --> 00:25:38.550
Never been anything
like that before.

00:25:38.550 --> 00:25:41.640
Wages-- or, about real
wages-- are about 15%

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:43.710
below what they were in 1973.

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:49.660
In fact, they're back at the
level of around 1964 or so.

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:51.730
Depends a little
how you measure.

00:25:51.730 --> 00:25:53.920
Incomes, to the extent
that they stay up,

00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:56.630
are staying up because people
are just working a lot more.

00:25:56.630 --> 00:25:58.450
So the typical
American family is

00:25:58.450 --> 00:26:02.860
putting in about 15
weeks a year more of work

00:26:02.860 --> 00:26:05.840
than it did 20 years
ago to try to keep it

00:26:05.840 --> 00:26:10.400
at a stagnating or
a declining level.

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:12.340
If you're interested
in data about this,

00:26:12.340 --> 00:26:15.640
the best general database
about this kind of stuff--

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:17.680
also written quite clearly--

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:18.980
comes out every two years.

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:20.688
It's called The State
of Working America.

00:26:20.688 --> 00:26:22.480
The Economic Policy
Institute just

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:29.410
came out with their 1998, '99
edition just a couple of weeks

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:33.250
ago, which can fill
you in on details

00:26:33.250 --> 00:26:35.890
up to the current evidence.

00:26:35.890 --> 00:26:37.930
Well, let me put
off for a moment

00:26:37.930 --> 00:26:41.765
how this return relates to
the fate of the three pillars.

00:26:41.765 --> 00:26:43.390
But the point that
I'm trying to stress

00:26:43.390 --> 00:26:47.200
is that all of this stuff has
to do with real human beings

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:47.980
and their fate.

00:26:47.980 --> 00:26:51.590
It's not a pleasant story.

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:55.030
And when we bring in the
fate of future generations

00:26:55.030 --> 00:27:00.330
who don't have a vote, as
it's called in the market,

00:27:00.330 --> 00:27:03.060
in the very restricted
market systems that exist--

00:27:03.060 --> 00:27:06.910
if we bring them in, then
the story is even uglier.

00:27:06.910 --> 00:27:09.280
Well, that's the first detour.

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:13.450
The second one-- and I will
get back to the topic--

00:27:13.450 --> 00:27:16.210
has to do with the norms
and the conventions

00:27:16.210 --> 00:27:19.300
for discussing all of this,
for discussing world order.

00:27:19.300 --> 00:27:21.310
And there are some
norms and conventions.

00:27:21.310 --> 00:27:22.810
And if you depart
from them, you're

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:25.480
kind of not part
of the discussion.

00:27:25.480 --> 00:27:30.280
The norms and the conventions
are that the goals,

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:33.550
the intentions, the purposes,
as they're sometimes called,

00:27:33.550 --> 00:27:37.120
of the United States and
its leaders are high-minded,

00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:39.310
sincere, or benign--

00:27:39.310 --> 00:27:41.020
other good things.

00:27:41.020 --> 00:27:44.920
And that, crucially, is
true independently a fact.

00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:48.280
And it is understood to be
true independently of fact.

00:27:48.280 --> 00:27:52.780
So for example, the very same
books and scholarly articles

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:56.980
and so on that pronounce
that as a truth often

00:27:56.980 --> 00:28:01.330
also are dedicated to showing
that it's refuted by the facts.

00:28:01.330 --> 00:28:04.560
And that's called an
irony or a departure

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:09.130
of some exogenous factor
or something or other.

00:28:09.130 --> 00:28:10.970
That's pretty standard.

00:28:10.970 --> 00:28:15.190
So indeed, the thesis is
refuted overwhelmingly

00:28:15.190 --> 00:28:17.350
by the internal
documentary record--

00:28:17.350 --> 00:28:20.020
the doc record of planning--
and even more dramatically

00:28:20.020 --> 00:28:22.420
by the historical
record of practice.

00:28:22.420 --> 00:28:23.680
But it doesn't matter.

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:25.960
The principle remains true.

00:28:25.960 --> 00:28:28.810
And it provides the
framework of discussion.

00:28:28.810 --> 00:28:32.290
And one part of a good education
is to understand that--

00:28:32.290 --> 00:28:35.470
to get that
internalized enough so

00:28:35.470 --> 00:28:38.020
you can then take
part in the debate,

00:28:38.020 --> 00:28:41.260
which proceeds in this fashion.

00:28:41.260 --> 00:28:48.440
It's reminiscent of medieval and
early modern theological dogma.

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:50.710
And interestingly, it's
sometimes even presented

00:28:50.710 --> 00:28:52.840
in those terms, especially
in what's called

00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:56.470
realist international theory.

00:28:56.470 --> 00:28:59.350
However, although the
comparison comes to mind,

00:28:59.350 --> 00:29:00.940
I think it's unfair.

00:29:00.940 --> 00:29:03.880
It's kind of an insult to
me to medieval theology.

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:04.720
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:04.720 --> 00:29:06.011
I mean that quite seriously.

00:29:06.011 --> 00:29:07.760
And I'll explain why
if you're interested.

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:09.640
But let me put it aside.

00:29:09.640 --> 00:29:11.410
Anyway, it has
something of the flavor

00:29:11.410 --> 00:29:13.750
of a theological principle.

00:29:13.750 --> 00:29:16.700
Notice that these principles
don't apply to anyone else.

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:20.230
So for example, nobody pays any
attention to the high-minded

00:29:20.230 --> 00:29:24.940
rhetoric of Stalin's
constitution or the exalted

00:29:24.940 --> 00:29:28.300
rhetoric that accompanies what
those who carry it out call

00:29:28.300 --> 00:29:33.100
humanitarian interventions, of
which the most obvious case is

00:29:33.100 --> 00:29:35.230
in this century, I suppose--
at least if you judge

00:29:35.230 --> 00:29:36.370
by the rhetoric--

00:29:36.370 --> 00:29:39.490
are Mussolini's
invasion of Ethiopia,

00:29:39.490 --> 00:29:43.570
the Japanese invasion of
Manchuria and North China,

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:47.500
and Hitler's invasion
of Czechoslovakia.

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:53.260
The rhetoric was very impressive
and full of humanitarian ideals

00:29:53.260 --> 00:29:55.090
and noble purposes and so on.

00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:57.860
And like most propaganda,
it wasn't totally false.

00:29:57.860 --> 00:30:00.670
Like you'll find
fragments of truth in it.

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:05.680
So for example, Hitler was
invited in by the president

00:30:05.680 --> 00:30:06.710
of Slovakia.

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:08.380
And the Japanese
actually had one

00:30:08.380 --> 00:30:12.010
of the leading Chinese
nationalists as a puppet

00:30:12.010 --> 00:30:13.850
and so on.

00:30:13.850 --> 00:30:15.920
But we don't pay any
attention to that stuff.

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:19.360
We don't regard that as
evidence of benign intentions

00:30:19.360 --> 00:30:21.190
in those and other cases.

00:30:21.190 --> 00:30:25.160
The principles hold only
at home, not anywhere else.

00:30:25.160 --> 00:30:28.590
And here they hold,
irrespective of the facts,

00:30:28.590 --> 00:30:32.080
and often in the
very same documents,

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:34.870
say, scholarly
articles, which present

00:30:34.870 --> 00:30:36.970
the facts to refute them.

00:30:36.970 --> 00:30:39.010
That's kind of misleading
to give examples

00:30:39.010 --> 00:30:42.280
because that would
understate the uniformity

00:30:42.280 --> 00:30:43.810
and the extremism.

00:30:43.810 --> 00:30:45.910
And it would be misleading,
quote, "the press."

00:30:45.910 --> 00:30:49.210
So I won't because
that's too easy.

00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:51.040
Let me just give
one example which

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:54.150
happens to be the last one
that I looked at last night

00:30:54.150 --> 00:30:57.010
in a current Journal of
International Affairs.

00:30:57.010 --> 00:30:59.830
And it's kind of an interesting
case and a very good scholar.

00:30:59.830 --> 00:31:02.380
Highly critical of US
government policies, which

00:31:02.380 --> 00:31:04.450
makes it more interesting--

00:31:04.450 --> 00:31:06.490
this article, too.

00:31:06.490 --> 00:31:10.150
It's worth examining, not
only because it illustrates

00:31:10.150 --> 00:31:17.050
the conventions, but because
of what we discover when

00:31:17.050 --> 00:31:19.480
we look a little more closely--

00:31:19.480 --> 00:31:21.070
not only about
contemporary history

00:31:21.070 --> 00:31:22.870
but also about ourselves--

00:31:22.870 --> 00:31:25.990
what we do, how we deal with it.

00:31:25.990 --> 00:31:29.200
The record is particularly
instructive in this case,

00:31:29.200 --> 00:31:32.470
because the Cold War is
almost totally irrelevant.

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:34.270
This goes back a long
way and never has

00:31:34.270 --> 00:31:36.710
been any Cold War relevant.

00:31:36.710 --> 00:31:38.410
And it's been recognized.

00:31:38.410 --> 00:31:40.630
The institutions of
power and decision making

00:31:40.630 --> 00:31:42.820
remain unchanged.

00:31:42.820 --> 00:31:45.659
From that we conclude,
if we were rational,

00:31:45.659 --> 00:31:47.950
that it's likely to be a
pretty good predictor of where

00:31:47.950 --> 00:31:50.200
things are going to
go, at least if we

00:31:50.200 --> 00:31:51.640
allow them to go that way.

00:31:59.690 --> 00:32:02.890
The article is by a well-known
Middle East scholar.

00:32:02.890 --> 00:32:06.220
He's also a leading policy
analyst at the Council

00:32:06.220 --> 00:32:08.260
on Foreign Relations
and elsewhere.

00:32:08.260 --> 00:32:10.652
He's been highly
critical of US policy

00:32:10.652 --> 00:32:12.610
toward the Middle East,
which is his specialty.

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:14.550
The article is, too.

00:32:14.550 --> 00:32:16.450
And he gives a
recommendation, which in fact

00:32:16.450 --> 00:32:17.802
is highlighted by the journal.

00:32:17.802 --> 00:32:20.260
So it's like the most important
part of the recommendation.

00:32:20.260 --> 00:32:22.690
It says, "The United
States should more

00:32:22.690 --> 00:32:26.440
energetically promote
central US foreign policy

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:30.820
themes, especially freedom,
democracy, and human rights."

00:32:30.820 --> 00:32:35.440
Notice these are central
US foreign policy themes.

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:37.960
No argument or
evidence is presented

00:32:37.960 --> 00:32:41.590
to establish that fact
because none is needed.

00:32:41.590 --> 00:32:43.930
That is a theological dogma.

00:32:43.930 --> 00:32:45.771
It's like "God is
great" or something.

00:32:45.771 --> 00:32:46.270
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:46.270 --> 00:32:48.700
You don't have to present
evidence for that.

00:32:48.700 --> 00:32:50.230
It's just a truth.

00:32:50.230 --> 00:32:54.670
Then the article proceeds, after
having established the truth.

00:32:54.670 --> 00:32:55.840
And it goes on--

00:32:55.840 --> 00:32:57.580
and this is quite typical--

00:32:57.580 --> 00:33:00.880
to describe how the United
States has consistently

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:05.050
acted contrary to its
central foreign policy themes

00:33:05.050 --> 00:33:05.890
in the Middle East.

00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:13.750
The author then goes on
to say, I'm quoting now,

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:15.570
"There is no doubt."

00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:18.310
Not only is this true,
but, "There is no doubt

00:33:18.310 --> 00:33:22.330
that American foreign policy
does adopt a separate standard

00:33:22.330 --> 00:33:25.030
for the Middle East when
it comes to such values

00:33:25.030 --> 00:33:27.295
as democracy and human rights."

00:33:27.295 --> 00:33:29.170
Well, to show that
there's a double standard,

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:30.640
you do need evidence.

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:33.010
So he presents some evidence.

00:33:33.010 --> 00:33:35.140
The evidence is
that, as he shows,

00:33:35.140 --> 00:33:37.810
the United States does
not uphold such values

00:33:37.810 --> 00:33:40.420
in the Middle East, which
is the area of his expertise

00:33:40.420 --> 00:33:41.470
and attention.

00:33:41.470 --> 00:33:44.350
But it does uphold
them elsewhere,

00:33:44.350 --> 00:33:49.930
strikingly, he points out, for
major Muslim states if they're

00:33:49.930 --> 00:33:51.240
not in the Middle East.

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:53.200
So that makes the
point very clear.

00:33:53.200 --> 00:33:55.960
And the most important case,
of course, as he notes,

00:33:55.960 --> 00:33:58.630
is Indonesia, the
largest Muslim state,

00:33:58.630 --> 00:34:03.250
which he cites, to establish
the certainty of the judgment

00:34:03.250 --> 00:34:05.800
that the United States
has a separate standard

00:34:05.800 --> 00:34:07.210
for the Middle East.

00:34:07.210 --> 00:34:09.880
Well, breaking with
the convention,

00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:13.810
let's look at the dogma
keeping to the single and most

00:34:13.810 --> 00:34:17.050
important example cited, namely,
Indonesia, where the United

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:21.280
States upholds its values of
democracy and human rights.

00:34:21.280 --> 00:34:26.170
Well, very much like Hitler,
Mussolini, and Japan,

00:34:26.170 --> 00:34:28.780
this doctrine isn't
totally false.

00:34:28.780 --> 00:34:35.230
So on May 20, 1998,
so that's last May,

00:34:35.230 --> 00:34:38.380
the United States did
make its first statement

00:34:38.380 --> 00:34:42.370
in favor of democracy
in Indonesia.

00:34:42.370 --> 00:34:45.429
Secretary of State
Albright on last May 20,

00:34:45.429 --> 00:34:49.210
called upon President
Suharto to resign and provide

00:34:49.210 --> 00:34:51.880
for a democratic transition.

00:34:51.880 --> 00:34:54.100
A few hours later, he resigned--

00:34:54.100 --> 00:34:56.462
maybe coincidence,
maybe it tells you

00:34:56.462 --> 00:34:57.795
something about power relations.

00:34:57.795 --> 00:34:58.295
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:58.295 --> 00:35:01.210
You can figure that
out for yourself.

00:35:01.210 --> 00:35:05.500
So last May, we
called for democracy.

00:35:05.500 --> 00:35:10.120
From the time that Suharto
took power in October, 1965,

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:15.610
until May, 1998, this
gentleman was "our kind

00:35:15.610 --> 00:35:18.800
of guy," as the Clinton
administration called him,

00:35:18.800 --> 00:35:21.220
while he was massacring
and torturing

00:35:21.220 --> 00:35:28.660
and robbing in a quite
impressive fashion--

00:35:28.660 --> 00:35:32.620
kind of one of the world
records of the modern period.

00:35:32.620 --> 00:35:35.200
The Clinton administration
even went so far as

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:39.310
to suspend its
congressionally-mandated review

00:35:39.310 --> 00:35:43.060
of Indonesia's appalling
labor practices,

00:35:43.060 --> 00:35:46.600
while at the same time praising
Indonesia for bringing them

00:35:46.600 --> 00:35:50.070
into closer conformity with
international standards.

00:35:50.070 --> 00:35:51.710
And the Clinton
administration also

00:35:51.710 --> 00:35:53.450
avoided congressional
restrictions

00:35:53.450 --> 00:35:56.180
on military support
for "our kind of guy"--

00:35:56.180 --> 00:35:59.270
found various ways to pay
for it out of other pockets

00:35:59.270 --> 00:36:00.860
and that sort of thing.

00:36:00.860 --> 00:36:04.460
But it is true that last May,
the United States underwent

00:36:04.460 --> 00:36:07.700
a religious conversion, and
for the first time called

00:36:07.700 --> 00:36:10.819
for a democracy in Indonesia.

00:36:10.819 --> 00:36:12.860
The force of the comparison
with the Middle East,

00:36:12.860 --> 00:36:15.260
however, is somewhat
weakened by the fact

00:36:15.260 --> 00:36:18.620
that the same conversion
occurred there at pretty much

00:36:18.620 --> 00:36:20.180
the same time.

00:36:20.180 --> 00:36:24.170
In December, 1998, Secretary
of State Albright announced,

00:36:24.170 --> 00:36:27.590
I'm quoting, "We have
come to the determination

00:36:27.590 --> 00:36:30.020
that the Iraqi
people would benefit

00:36:30.020 --> 00:36:33.530
if they had a government that
really represented them."

00:36:33.530 --> 00:36:37.310
So in both the Middle
East and Indonesia,

00:36:37.310 --> 00:36:39.650
contrary to the thesis,
the United States

00:36:39.650 --> 00:36:43.370
underwent a religious conversion
in favor of the central themes

00:36:43.370 --> 00:36:44.800
of its foreign policy--

00:36:44.800 --> 00:36:49.010
at least on paper, you can
evaluate the accuracy--

00:36:49.010 --> 00:36:52.160
about at the same
time last year.

00:36:52.160 --> 00:36:55.850
In the case of Iraq,
before that, the US policy

00:36:55.850 --> 00:36:58.970
was to support what was
called an iron fist.

00:36:58.970 --> 00:37:01.730
The iron fist was
Saddam Hussein.

00:37:01.730 --> 00:37:03.770
For a long time,
he was "our kind

00:37:03.770 --> 00:37:07.880
of guy," right through
his most murderous crimes

00:37:07.880 --> 00:37:10.310
and most monstrous crimes.

00:37:10.310 --> 00:37:14.360
After he committed his
first true offense,

00:37:14.360 --> 00:37:16.290
the thesis shifted.

00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:17.630
We still wanted an iron fist.

00:37:17.630 --> 00:37:19.850
But it had to be
a clone, someone

00:37:19.850 --> 00:37:23.900
who would rule with an iron
fist just as Saddam Hussein had

00:37:23.900 --> 00:37:27.140
done during the period when
he was "our kind of guy."

00:37:27.140 --> 00:37:30.620
That was pretty much
official policy.

00:37:30.620 --> 00:37:33.200
But on paper, at least,
it's now changed.

00:37:33.200 --> 00:37:35.180
Because as of last
December, we discovered

00:37:35.180 --> 00:37:38.150
that they would benefit
from a government that

00:37:38.150 --> 00:37:39.860
represented them.

00:37:39.860 --> 00:37:42.530
So in 1998 then,
the United States

00:37:42.530 --> 00:37:45.390
did announce support
for democracy,

00:37:45.390 --> 00:37:48.210
but both in the Middle
East and Indonesia.

00:37:48.210 --> 00:37:50.510
Well, what does this tell
us about the central themes

00:37:50.510 --> 00:37:52.250
of US foreign policy?

00:37:52.250 --> 00:37:54.800
Answer-- it tells us
nothing whatsoever,

00:37:54.800 --> 00:37:57.020
because these are
doctrinal truths.

00:37:57.020 --> 00:38:00.020
And therefore, facts
are simply irrelevant--

00:38:00.020 --> 00:38:02.330
like, you know, God
is great and merciful.

00:38:02.330 --> 00:38:04.850
You don't look at
factual truths.

00:38:04.850 --> 00:38:07.070
In fact, if you look
more-- well, put it aside.

00:38:07.070 --> 00:38:08.300
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:09.590 --> 00:38:11.600
I don't want to take
too many detours.

00:38:11.600 --> 00:38:16.670
[CHUCKLES] Let's look a little
more closely at Indonesia,

00:38:16.670 --> 00:38:20.930
where there is no doubt that
these themes of democracy

00:38:20.930 --> 00:38:23.720
and human rights were
put into practice.

00:38:23.720 --> 00:38:27.710
There's a lot of concern right
now about the possibility

00:38:27.710 --> 00:38:34.250
that Indonesia may break up into
either a federation or maybe

00:38:34.250 --> 00:38:35.270
separate territories.

00:38:35.270 --> 00:38:38.299
And that prospect is
very much deplored.

00:38:38.299 --> 00:38:40.340
However, the objection to
it, wherever it may be,

00:38:40.340 --> 00:38:42.890
is certainly not an
objection of principle.

00:38:42.890 --> 00:38:45.110
And we can find that
out quite easily

00:38:45.110 --> 00:38:48.900
if we bother to look at
the irrelevant facts.

00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:53.570
So in 1958, that happened
to be official US policy--

00:38:53.570 --> 00:38:57.230
secret, but we now
know it was official US

00:38:57.230 --> 00:39:00.860
policy to break up Indonesia.

00:39:00.860 --> 00:39:03.830
And the United States,
in fact, carried out

00:39:03.830 --> 00:39:07.400
a major counterinsurgency--
well, insurgency operation--

00:39:07.400 --> 00:39:09.980
subversive operation--
probably the major one

00:39:09.980 --> 00:39:12.860
of the post Second
World War period

00:39:12.860 --> 00:39:15.355
to try to break up Indonesia.

00:39:15.355 --> 00:39:18.290
It supported a rebellion
in the outer islands.

00:39:18.290 --> 00:39:20.180
Those are the islands
where the oil is

00:39:20.180 --> 00:39:22.820
and where most US
investments were.

00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:26.090
And that was the goal,
to separate them off.

00:39:26.090 --> 00:39:32.630
And the reason was that
the national government was

00:39:32.630 --> 00:39:35.390
neutralist and
independent and, in fact,

00:39:35.390 --> 00:39:38.600
was far too democratic,
which was extremely

00:39:38.600 --> 00:39:40.850
worrisome to Washington.

00:39:40.850 --> 00:39:42.380
The government was--

00:39:42.380 --> 00:39:44.600
I'm not suggesting it
was a model democracy,

00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:48.380
but it was allowing parties
of the poor to function.

00:39:48.380 --> 00:39:52.129
There was a major party which
represented poor peasants.

00:39:52.129 --> 00:39:53.420
And it was allowed to function.

00:39:53.420 --> 00:39:55.940
And it was doing better and
better in each election.

00:39:55.940 --> 00:39:58.970
And that was appalling
news in Washington.

00:39:58.970 --> 00:40:02.940
If we look at the
internal record--

00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:04.940
most of which has been
suppressed, incidentally.

00:40:04.940 --> 00:40:06.350
But bits and pieces have leaked.

00:40:06.350 --> 00:40:08.630
And there's some good
scholarly studies of it--

00:40:08.630 --> 00:40:11.930
one, in fact, by
the leading founder

00:40:11.930 --> 00:40:18.020
of US/Southeast Asian studies,
George Kahin at Cornell.

00:40:18.020 --> 00:40:20.420
But there's also some
documents that have come out

00:40:20.420 --> 00:40:21.860
in the official channels.

00:40:21.860 --> 00:40:26.480
The fear was that the
government was, let's say,

00:40:26.480 --> 00:40:28.220
far too democratic.

00:40:28.220 --> 00:40:31.430
And it would be
impossible, as they put it,

00:40:31.430 --> 00:40:34.640
to suppress the
parties of the Left

00:40:34.640 --> 00:40:37.370
by ordinary democratic means.

00:40:37.370 --> 00:40:39.770
And therefore, they
would have to be--

00:40:39.770 --> 00:40:43.980
the word is "eliminated."

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:47.290
That's the joint chiefs.

00:40:47.290 --> 00:40:49.540
Meanwhile, the outer islands
would be separated off.

00:40:49.540 --> 00:40:51.340
So inside, what's left--

00:40:51.340 --> 00:40:54.610
bad guys have to be eliminated.

00:40:54.610 --> 00:40:56.270
The outer islands
will be separated,

00:40:56.270 --> 00:40:57.910
and the US will take them over.

00:40:57.910 --> 00:41:01.480
Well, the rebellion failed.

00:41:01.480 --> 00:41:06.610
But not before destroying
whatever fabric

00:41:06.610 --> 00:41:11.020
of a parliamentary democracy
existed in Indonesia.

00:41:11.020 --> 00:41:16.105
And now, after four years, we're
calling for it to be restored.

00:41:19.090 --> 00:41:21.670
After the failure
of the rebellion,

00:41:21.670 --> 00:41:24.190
the United States turned to
what is standard operating

00:41:24.190 --> 00:41:25.690
procedure when you
want to overthrow

00:41:25.690 --> 00:41:26.995
a civilian government--

00:41:29.862 --> 00:41:31.570
try to undermine the
civilian government,

00:41:31.570 --> 00:41:33.040
but support the military.

00:41:33.040 --> 00:41:34.660
Because they're the ones
who are going to overthrow

00:41:34.660 --> 00:41:35.618
the government for you.

00:41:35.618 --> 00:41:38.540
That's absolutely
standard, case after case.

00:41:38.540 --> 00:41:41.280
Pinochet is one example.

00:41:41.280 --> 00:41:45.660
The real story about what lies
behind Iran-Contra is the same.

00:41:45.660 --> 00:41:47.980
And we know it had nothing
to do with hostages

00:41:47.980 --> 00:41:51.130
because it was started when
there were no hostages.

00:41:51.130 --> 00:41:54.520
And it's just standard
operating procedure.

00:41:54.520 --> 00:41:56.260
In Indonesia, it worked.

00:41:56.260 --> 00:41:58.510
The US tried to undermine
the civilian government.

00:41:58.510 --> 00:42:00.155
It supported the military.

00:42:00.155 --> 00:42:00.870
It worked.

00:42:00.870 --> 00:42:05.080
By 1965, there was,
indeed, a military coup.

00:42:05.080 --> 00:42:07.000
Suharto took over.

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:11.320
An army-inspired
massacre then took place.

00:42:11.320 --> 00:42:14.500
The CIA described it
as one of the worst

00:42:14.500 --> 00:42:18.820
massacres of the 20th century,
comparable to Hitler, Stalin,

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:19.930
and Mao.

00:42:19.930 --> 00:42:22.540
It was fairly actively
reported in the United States.

00:42:22.540 --> 00:42:24.250
It was a staggering bloodbath.

00:42:24.250 --> 00:42:29.800
The New York Times called it
a "staggering mass slaughter."

00:42:29.800 --> 00:42:31.570
That was the Times' phrase.

00:42:31.570 --> 00:42:34.157
Time Magazine devoted
a whole section too,

00:42:34.157 --> 00:42:35.740
to what they called
the "boiling blood

00:42:35.740 --> 00:42:38.170
bath," in which hundreds
of thousands of people

00:42:38.170 --> 00:42:41.110
were massacred,
mostly poor peasants.

00:42:41.110 --> 00:42:45.250
And the leadership of
the poor peasant party

00:42:45.250 --> 00:42:47.020
was, indeed, eliminated.

00:42:47.020 --> 00:42:50.590
It was reported accurately
and with complete euphoria.

00:42:50.590 --> 00:42:53.610
There was such joy over it, that
it couldn't even be suppressed.

00:42:53.610 --> 00:42:55.360
It's kind had been
written out of history.

00:42:55.360 --> 00:42:58.480
But to go back and read
the journals of the time,

00:42:58.480 --> 00:43:02.650
the euphoria is total, across
the intellectual spectrum.

00:43:02.650 --> 00:43:07.190
Freedom House was
applauding and so on.

00:43:07.190 --> 00:43:10.660
The US government applauded.

00:43:10.660 --> 00:43:14.410
Secretary of Defense McNamara
testified before Congress

00:43:14.410 --> 00:43:18.580
that the military aid that
we'd given to the Indonesian

00:43:18.580 --> 00:43:21.040
military had paid dividends.

00:43:21.040 --> 00:43:24.050
In a private letter
to President Johnson,

00:43:24.050 --> 00:43:26.470
he was particularly
proud of the training

00:43:26.470 --> 00:43:28.810
that Indonesian military
officers received

00:43:28.810 --> 00:43:32.200
at American universities, where
they had gotten the right ideas

00:43:32.200 --> 00:43:36.100
about how to carry out a mass
slaughter comparable to Hitler,

00:43:36.100 --> 00:43:38.020
Stalin, and Mao.

00:43:38.020 --> 00:43:41.680
The country became a
paradise for investors.

00:43:41.680 --> 00:43:45.430
Suharto became the darling
of the United States--

00:43:45.430 --> 00:43:47.080
"our kind of guy."

00:43:47.080 --> 00:43:52.300
Remained so when he invaded East
Timor, wiped out maybe a third

00:43:52.300 --> 00:43:54.390
or a quarter of the population.

00:43:54.390 --> 00:43:57.850
And that continues
right into 1997.

00:43:57.850 --> 00:43:59.230
Then something went wrong.

00:43:59.230 --> 00:44:01.960
He apparently committed a crime.

00:44:01.960 --> 00:44:03.490
What crime is it?

00:44:03.490 --> 00:44:06.490
Well, in fact, the crime,
if you look closely,

00:44:06.490 --> 00:44:08.500
which led to the
religious conversion

00:44:08.500 --> 00:44:11.980
in favor of a democratic
transition here--

00:44:11.980 --> 00:44:14.740
there were actually two
crimes, two standard ones.

00:44:14.740 --> 00:44:19.210
One crime is, he start dragging
his feet about IMF orders.

00:44:19.210 --> 00:44:24.370
The IMF rules were imposing
extremely serious hardships

00:44:24.370 --> 00:44:25.930
on the people of Indonesia.

00:44:25.930 --> 00:44:29.110
And Suharto was kind of
slow about following orders.

00:44:29.110 --> 00:44:30.830
Crime number one.

00:44:30.830 --> 00:44:32.980
The second crime is
he just lost control.

00:44:32.980 --> 00:44:35.920
There was a democratic uprising.

00:44:35.920 --> 00:44:38.880
And the army wasn't
backing him anymore.

00:44:38.880 --> 00:44:42.460
And at that point, he's useless.

00:44:42.460 --> 00:44:47.980
So therefore, he got
the advice to permit

00:44:47.980 --> 00:44:50.920
a democratic transition, in
which he handed power over

00:44:50.920 --> 00:44:54.490
to his handpicked
vice president.

00:44:54.490 --> 00:44:57.430
Well, that's the moment at
which the United States,

00:44:57.430 --> 00:45:02.230
after 40 years, began,
at least on paper,

00:45:02.230 --> 00:45:06.700
to implement its central
theme of its policy

00:45:06.700 --> 00:45:09.250
in the area where there is
no doubt that it has always

00:45:09.250 --> 00:45:13.600
implemented it, remember, as
distinct from the Middle East.

00:45:13.600 --> 00:45:16.390
By committing those
two crimes, Suharto

00:45:16.390 --> 00:45:19.050
followed a very classic path.

00:45:19.050 --> 00:45:22.480
Another recent example is
Mobutu, Saddam Hussein,

00:45:22.480 --> 00:45:26.929
Duvalier, Marcos,
Somoza, Trujillo.

00:45:26.929 --> 00:45:27.970
It's kind of a long list.

00:45:27.970 --> 00:45:32.030
The same two crimes are
the ones that matter.

00:45:32.030 --> 00:45:34.840
You stop following
orders, you lose control--

00:45:34.840 --> 00:45:38.800
you're out, and we're in favor
of a democratic transition.

00:45:38.800 --> 00:45:41.890
This happens
routinely, regularly.

00:45:41.890 --> 00:45:43.330
But it doesn't
teach you anything.

00:45:43.330 --> 00:45:45.100
You don't learn
anything about policy

00:45:45.100 --> 00:45:50.670
from that, because the nature of
policy is a theological dogma.

00:45:50.670 --> 00:45:53.200
And therefore, no evidence
tells you anything.

00:45:53.200 --> 00:45:56.826
Well, look a little
more closely at 1958.

00:45:56.826 --> 00:45:58.950
I still promise to get back
to the theme, if you're

00:45:58.950 --> 00:45:59.783
willing to hold out.

00:45:59.783 --> 00:46:00.720
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:00.720 --> 00:46:03.720
It's very revealing.

00:46:03.720 --> 00:46:08.470
In 1958, the National Security
Council remained planning--

00:46:08.470 --> 00:46:13.140
the body had then secret
meetings, now publicized,

00:46:13.140 --> 00:46:18.540
in which John Foster
Dulles, secretary of state,

00:46:18.540 --> 00:46:21.270
described the crises
on the world scene.

00:46:21.270 --> 00:46:22.920
He said there's
three major crises

00:46:22.920 --> 00:46:25.410
in the world scene in 1958.

00:46:25.410 --> 00:46:27.810
One was Indonesia.

00:46:27.810 --> 00:46:29.730
The second was Algeria.

00:46:29.730 --> 00:46:32.430
The third was the Middle East.

00:46:32.430 --> 00:46:35.730
Notice that they're all
Muslim countries, but there

00:46:35.730 --> 00:46:39.150
was no clash of
civilizations at that point.

00:46:39.150 --> 00:46:42.600
Those of you who are
respectable intellectuals

00:46:42.600 --> 00:46:44.970
will know that now we
have to soberly debate

00:46:44.970 --> 00:46:48.900
the clash of civilizations
because the Cold War pretext

00:46:48.900 --> 00:46:50.500
for intervention has collapsed.

00:46:50.500 --> 00:46:52.890
And we need what's
called a new paradigm.

00:46:52.890 --> 00:46:55.060
But then, we still
have that pretext.

00:46:55.060 --> 00:46:57.040
So we didn't need
a new paradigm.

00:46:57.040 --> 00:47:02.010
So the three major crises
were Indonesia, Algeria,

00:47:02.010 --> 00:47:04.274
and the Middle East.

00:47:04.274 --> 00:47:06.190
The fact that they were
Muslims is irrelevant.

00:47:06.190 --> 00:47:09.890
But something isn't irrelevant--
namely, they all had oil.

00:47:09.890 --> 00:47:12.000
In fact, they were
all oil centers.

00:47:12.000 --> 00:47:16.800
And in fact, the effort to
cut off the outer islands

00:47:16.800 --> 00:47:21.300
was related to US concerns
over Middle East oil.

00:47:21.300 --> 00:47:24.180
There were some concerns that
it was getting out of control

00:47:24.180 --> 00:47:27.690
and they needed some temporary
supplements-- temporary,

00:47:27.690 --> 00:47:30.360
because Indonesia is nowhere
near the scale of the Middle

00:47:30.360 --> 00:47:32.340
East, but significant.

00:47:32.340 --> 00:47:34.650
Well, you look back
at what happened--

00:47:34.650 --> 00:47:37.710
and bear in mind that the
United States is a global power.

00:47:37.710 --> 00:47:41.310
So its policies are usually
carried out consistently

00:47:41.310 --> 00:47:42.820
in many different places.

00:47:42.820 --> 00:47:45.810
And a lot of other things
were happening in 1958.

00:47:45.810 --> 00:47:48.880
The subversion in Indonesia,
the rebellion in Indonesia,

00:47:48.880 --> 00:47:52.440
was the largest of the
clandestine operations-- maybe

00:47:52.440 --> 00:47:54.000
the largest ever.

00:47:54.000 --> 00:47:55.290
But there were others.

00:47:55.290 --> 00:47:58.590
In Burma, the United
States was moving

00:47:58.590 --> 00:48:00.240
to overthrow the
regime supporting

00:48:00.240 --> 00:48:04.380
a Chinese nationalist army
that had partially taken refuge

00:48:04.380 --> 00:48:07.540
there, and that the United
States was supporting there.

00:48:07.540 --> 00:48:10.410
I won't go into the details, but
that led Burma-- at that time,

00:48:10.410 --> 00:48:12.150
had an elected government--

00:48:12.150 --> 00:48:15.420
that led to a military
coup, the installation

00:48:15.420 --> 00:48:19.080
of the current military regime
in Burma-- one of the most

00:48:19.080 --> 00:48:20.410
brutal in the world.

00:48:20.410 --> 00:48:24.540
It also was instrumental in
making Burma the leading heroin

00:48:24.540 --> 00:48:28.410
producer in the world, a
position it now holds--

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:29.940
and a real horror story.

00:48:29.940 --> 00:48:33.180
The state of Massachusetts
now has a kind of boycott.

00:48:33.180 --> 00:48:35.460
We can trace that
right back to 1958--

00:48:35.460 --> 00:48:38.520
right parallel to when
we were carrying out

00:48:38.520 --> 00:48:42.660
the central themes of our
foreign policy in Indonesia.

00:48:42.660 --> 00:48:46.676
Another case right
next door was Cambodia.

00:48:46.676 --> 00:48:48.300
Also had a neutralist
government, which

00:48:48.300 --> 00:48:49.890
the United States didn't like.

00:48:49.890 --> 00:48:52.920
The US was supporting
an attack on it.

00:48:52.920 --> 00:48:55.270
That attack didn't work in 1958.

00:48:55.270 --> 00:48:59.040
But it did work in 1970--
the same forces overthrowing

00:48:59.040 --> 00:49:00.750
Prince Sihanouk's government.

00:49:00.750 --> 00:49:06.240
That led to the huge US
bombing, the Khmer Rouge,

00:49:06.240 --> 00:49:07.730
everything that followed--

00:49:07.730 --> 00:49:09.000
need not talk about that.

00:49:11.515 --> 00:49:14.400
A third place where something
was going on in 1958

00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:16.740
was, again, right
nearby in Laos.

00:49:16.740 --> 00:49:20.730
Laos had its first and
last free election in 1958,

00:49:20.730 --> 00:49:23.190
came out the wrong way,
party to the Left won.

00:49:23.190 --> 00:49:25.170
The US wouldn't accept that.

00:49:25.170 --> 00:49:26.550
Military coup took place.

00:49:26.550 --> 00:49:30.150
The US installed what was called
a "pro-Western neutralist."

00:49:30.150 --> 00:49:31.250
And he wasn't good enough.

00:49:31.250 --> 00:49:34.650
So an ultra-Right
general was put in.

00:49:34.650 --> 00:49:36.330
That led to a total disaster.

00:49:36.330 --> 00:49:39.020
It sort of paved the
way and actually came

00:49:39.020 --> 00:49:42.390
pretty close to a world war.

00:49:42.390 --> 00:49:45.090
It ended up with the US carrying
out the heaviest bombing

00:49:45.090 --> 00:49:48.090
in history against a
defenseless peasant

00:49:48.090 --> 00:49:50.220
society in northern Laos.

00:49:50.220 --> 00:49:52.320
And the effects of
that are still with us.

00:49:52.320 --> 00:49:53.670
But you wouldn't read it.

00:49:53.670 --> 00:49:55.500
You can't even read it
in the US newspaper.

00:49:55.500 --> 00:49:58.440
That's a very good article on
it in The Wall Street Journal

00:49:58.440 --> 00:50:04.080
by their veteran Asia
correspondent about a year

00:50:04.080 --> 00:50:05.100
and a half ago.

00:50:05.100 --> 00:50:06.766
Unfortunately, it's
the Asian edition

00:50:06.766 --> 00:50:07.890
of The Wall Street Journal.

00:50:07.890 --> 00:50:09.725
They didn't have it
in the US edition.

00:50:09.725 --> 00:50:10.350
[SOFT LAUGHTER]

00:50:10.350 --> 00:50:11.700
But it's quite a good article.

00:50:11.700 --> 00:50:13.530
He gives an estimate of--

00:50:13.530 --> 00:50:17.610
there's unknown numbers of
unexploded bomblets just

00:50:17.610 --> 00:50:19.500
littering the Plain of Jars.

00:50:19.500 --> 00:50:21.480
Hundreds of millions
of them were dropped.

00:50:21.480 --> 00:50:23.790
They are much worse
than landmines.

00:50:23.790 --> 00:50:25.770
These are not aimed at property.

00:50:25.770 --> 00:50:28.290
They do nothing against a truck.

00:50:28.290 --> 00:50:30.330
They're aimed to kill people.

00:50:30.330 --> 00:50:32.130
They're murder weapons.

00:50:32.130 --> 00:50:35.870
They're little, colorful
things that a kid can pick up

00:50:35.870 --> 00:50:38.430
or a farmer can
hit or something.

00:50:38.430 --> 00:50:42.690
They had a failure-to-explode
rate of about 20% to 30%,

00:50:42.690 --> 00:50:45.330
according to their
manufacturer, Honeywell.

00:50:45.330 --> 00:50:48.050
And crummy as
technology may be, it's

00:50:48.050 --> 00:50:51.020
hard to believe that
that wasn't built in.

00:50:51.020 --> 00:50:52.210
But maybe.

00:50:52.210 --> 00:50:55.500
The technologists among you
can assess that likelihood.

00:50:55.500 --> 00:50:57.860
Anyway, they're supposed
to have a 20% to 30%

00:50:57.860 --> 00:50:59.540
failure to explode
rate, which means

00:50:59.540 --> 00:51:02.750
they're around all the time
as anti-personnel weapons.

00:51:02.750 --> 00:51:04.740
And they're still there--
huge numbers of them.

00:51:07.492 --> 00:51:09.200
According The Wall
Street Journal report,

00:51:09.200 --> 00:51:13.080
the casualties may range
up to 20,000 a year,

00:51:13.080 --> 00:51:15.140
of which more than
half are deaths.

00:51:15.140 --> 00:51:16.910
Other numbers are less.

00:51:16.910 --> 00:51:20.745
But since nobody is counting,
nobody really knows.

00:51:20.745 --> 00:51:22.370
But numbers of that
kind are considered

00:51:22.370 --> 00:51:24.590
not unreasonable by
The Wall Street Journal

00:51:24.590 --> 00:51:25.760
in its Asia edition.

00:51:28.360 --> 00:51:30.170
There is a mine-clearing group--

00:51:30.170 --> 00:51:33.530
British-based mine
advisory group-- civilian--

00:51:33.530 --> 00:51:36.320
but based, ultimately,
in the British army.

00:51:36.320 --> 00:51:39.790
And other countries
have come in.

00:51:39.790 --> 00:51:42.410
As the British press
reports, the United States

00:51:42.410 --> 00:51:47.150
is conspicuous by its absence
in the mine-clearing operation.

00:51:47.150 --> 00:51:50.390
And furthermore, the right-wing
British press is bitterly

00:51:50.390 --> 00:51:53.210
complaining over the fact
that the United States,

00:51:53.210 --> 00:52:01.430
the Pentagon, refuses to provide
what are called render-harmless

00:52:01.430 --> 00:52:03.290
procedures--

00:52:03.290 --> 00:52:05.900
procedures that would
defuse the mines

00:52:05.900 --> 00:52:09.050
so they wouldn't kill the people
who are trying to clear them.

00:52:09.050 --> 00:52:10.092
That's a military secret.

00:52:10.092 --> 00:52:12.675
And in fact, the whole thing is
a secret in the United States.

00:52:12.675 --> 00:52:15.230
You have to work pretty hard
to find out anything about it.

00:52:15.230 --> 00:52:15.860
That's a fact.

00:52:15.860 --> 00:52:17.900
So that's Laos.

00:52:17.900 --> 00:52:20.060
Another case, in
1958 was Vietnam.

00:52:20.060 --> 00:52:22.250
I won't even talk about that.

00:52:22.250 --> 00:52:24.530
In each case,
including all of these,

00:52:24.530 --> 00:52:28.370
including Indonesia,
the US interventions,

00:52:28.370 --> 00:52:33.140
which were significant,
led to hideous atrocities

00:52:33.140 --> 00:52:35.870
and, in fact, destroyed
the basis of democracy

00:52:35.870 --> 00:52:39.890
and, in fact, were, to a large
extent, driven by that purpose,

00:52:39.890 --> 00:52:42.150
as in several of the
cases I've mentioned.

00:52:42.150 --> 00:52:44.000
However, that doesn't
influence the dogma.

00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:46.220
It's still correct.

00:52:46.220 --> 00:52:49.820
There is still no doubt
that, in the Middle East

00:52:49.820 --> 00:52:53.300
only, the United States doesn't
live up to its high ideals,

00:52:53.300 --> 00:52:56.250
but does in these places.

00:52:56.250 --> 00:52:59.780
Well, if you take a little
more closer look at 1958,

00:52:59.780 --> 00:53:01.250
I won't go into this--

00:53:01.250 --> 00:53:02.570
bring it up later if you like--

00:53:02.570 --> 00:53:06.380
but the other major
case was, in fact, Iraq.

00:53:06.380 --> 00:53:08.615
Iraq had a nationalist
revolution,

00:53:08.615 --> 00:53:11.600
and it was pulling out
of the US Anglo-American

00:53:11.600 --> 00:53:13.580
[? condominium ?] over oil.

00:53:13.580 --> 00:53:16.850
That was going on right in 1958,
and it was a major phenomenon.

00:53:16.850 --> 00:53:18.890
If you look at the
documentary record then,

00:53:18.890 --> 00:53:20.870
you get a pretty good
explanation of everything

00:53:20.870 --> 00:53:23.440
that's going out until today.

00:53:23.440 --> 00:53:26.570
OK, let me drop that.

00:53:26.570 --> 00:53:28.580
One of the things that's
going on today, which

00:53:28.580 --> 00:53:31.140
is quite striking and
leads me to the topic,

00:53:31.140 --> 00:53:36.530
finally, is a kind of a
minor aspect of the bombing

00:53:36.530 --> 00:53:39.560
last December--
the US-UK bombing.

00:53:39.560 --> 00:53:43.010
One interesting aspect
of it was that it

00:53:43.010 --> 00:53:48.410
is in blatant and flat
violation of the UN

00:53:48.410 --> 00:53:51.599
Charter and international law.

00:53:51.599 --> 00:53:53.390
International law--
that's one of the three

00:53:53.390 --> 00:53:54.830
pillars of world order.

00:53:54.830 --> 00:53:58.280
The basic principle
of the UN Charter

00:53:58.280 --> 00:54:01.430
is that the threat or use of
force in international affairs

00:54:01.430 --> 00:54:05.060
is banned, except under highly
restricted circumstances, which

00:54:05.060 --> 00:54:08.510
don't apply, or if specifically
authorized by the Security

00:54:08.510 --> 00:54:09.200
Council.

00:54:09.200 --> 00:54:12.110
Otherwise, banned--
threat or use of force.

00:54:12.110 --> 00:54:17.840
There is no serious doubt
that the US and UK just flatly

00:54:17.840 --> 00:54:18.830
ignored that.

00:54:18.830 --> 00:54:21.410
They didn't try to get UN
Security Council authorization

00:54:21.410 --> 00:54:23.600
for the simple reason they
knew they'd never get it.

00:54:23.600 --> 00:54:25.164
So therefore, they just bombed.

00:54:25.164 --> 00:54:27.080
Now in the United States,
there is essentially

00:54:27.080 --> 00:54:28.790
no discussion of this.

00:54:28.790 --> 00:54:31.680
I've done a fairly
extensive review.

00:54:31.680 --> 00:54:34.040
And where it's discussed,
as it occasionally is,

00:54:34.040 --> 00:54:37.700
it's considered a kind of a
technicality, the reason being,

00:54:37.700 --> 00:54:42.260
if a reason is ever given, that
we cannot allow others to veto

00:54:42.260 --> 00:54:46.100
our policy decisions, as
required by the UN charter

00:54:46.100 --> 00:54:49.430
and international law.

00:54:49.430 --> 00:54:53.060
Others must stand by
that, but we can't.

00:54:53.060 --> 00:54:57.560
That is unchallenged, as
far as I can discover.

00:54:57.560 --> 00:54:59.870
Interesting exercise--
try to find some challenge

00:54:59.870 --> 00:55:00.950
to that doctrine.

00:55:00.950 --> 00:55:02.480
I haven't found it.

00:55:02.480 --> 00:55:03.650
There is some in England.

00:55:03.650 --> 00:55:05.420
And there's a lot elsewhere.

00:55:05.420 --> 00:55:10.550
So for example, in India,
the Association of Jurists

00:55:10.550 --> 00:55:13.880
has a case before the world
court condemning the US

00:55:13.880 --> 00:55:15.790
and Britain for war crimes.

00:55:15.790 --> 00:55:18.170
You won't read
about that either.

00:55:18.170 --> 00:55:21.020
But here it's not discussed,
because it's taken for granted

00:55:21.020 --> 00:55:22.190
that that's correct.

00:55:22.190 --> 00:55:27.230
We cannot submit ourselves to
international law or the UN

00:55:27.230 --> 00:55:31.430
Charter because we are a
violent, lawless, criminal,

00:55:31.430 --> 00:55:33.980
rogue state, and that
is right and just.

00:55:33.980 --> 00:55:35.380
That's what it means.

00:55:35.380 --> 00:55:39.650
And there is near universal
endorsement of that principle,

00:55:39.650 --> 00:55:42.830
among educated sectors
at least, which

00:55:42.830 --> 00:55:45.050
should be taken as a
warning by the world,

00:55:45.050 --> 00:55:47.660
and indeed is taken as a
warning by the world, which

00:55:47.660 --> 00:55:50.760
doesn't like it, but
can't do much about it.

00:55:50.760 --> 00:55:54.010
We're the ones who can
do something about it.

00:55:54.010 --> 00:55:56.900
And at least, judging
articulate sectors,

00:55:56.900 --> 00:55:58.440
educated sectors support it.

00:55:58.440 --> 00:56:00.830
So they're not going to
do anything about it.

00:56:00.830 --> 00:56:05.180
The official stand during
the bombing in December

00:56:05.180 --> 00:56:09.590
was, quoted, "that we prefer
to act through our allies,

00:56:09.590 --> 00:56:13.430
but will resort to force
alone if we have to."

00:56:13.430 --> 00:56:15.350
Notice that we
don't even "prefer"

00:56:15.350 --> 00:56:17.330
to act through the
United Nations,

00:56:17.330 --> 00:56:20.975
as required by international
law in the Charter.

00:56:20.975 --> 00:56:27.030
We join our allies if they're
willing to, but UN is out.

00:56:27.030 --> 00:56:28.970
In fact, the timing
of the bombing

00:56:28.970 --> 00:56:31.910
was presumably intended
as a slap in the face

00:56:31.910 --> 00:56:33.530
to the Security Council.

00:56:33.530 --> 00:56:36.920
The bombing was timed just as
the Security Council was being

00:56:36.920 --> 00:56:39.167
called to an emergency
session to deal

00:56:39.167 --> 00:56:40.250
with the question of Iraq.

00:56:40.250 --> 00:56:41.900
And it hadn't been notified.

00:56:41.900 --> 00:56:43.700
And if that didn't
make headlines here,

00:56:43.700 --> 00:56:45.281
people understood it elsewhere.

00:56:45.281 --> 00:56:47.030
Things like that don't
happen by accident.

00:56:47.030 --> 00:56:48.680
And they were understood.

00:56:48.680 --> 00:56:50.810
Well, that's accepted
across the spectrum.

00:56:50.810 --> 00:56:52.610
And it's accepted
in other cases, too.

00:56:52.610 --> 00:56:56.870
So take a look at the
Rambouillet meetings in Kosovo.

00:56:56.870 --> 00:57:00.050
There was a debate there
about just what to do.

00:57:00.050 --> 00:57:02.710
The debate was between the
United States and its allies

00:57:02.710 --> 00:57:03.980
and the NATO powers.

00:57:03.980 --> 00:57:05.420
Nobody else was around.

00:57:05.420 --> 00:57:08.990
And the debate was
over the wording--

00:57:08.990 --> 00:57:13.100
should the wording of
the NATO decision to bomb

00:57:13.100 --> 00:57:18.080
be stated as being "authorized"
by the United Nations

00:57:18.080 --> 00:57:21.460
or only being "endorsed"
by the United Nations?

00:57:21.460 --> 00:57:24.320
The US insisted on "endorse."

00:57:24.320 --> 00:57:26.120
As The New York Times
put it, it wanted

00:57:26.120 --> 00:57:29.780
to avoid the neuralgic
word "authorized,"

00:57:29.780 --> 00:57:33.680
which would entail that
international law has

00:57:33.680 --> 00:57:35.150
some significance.

00:57:35.150 --> 00:57:38.570
And the US won't accept that,
even at the rhetorical level.

00:57:38.570 --> 00:57:40.760
And as usual, the US won.

00:57:40.760 --> 00:57:44.870
So the most they get
to do is "endorse,"

00:57:44.870 --> 00:57:49.440
not "authorize,"
let alone "order,"

00:57:49.440 --> 00:57:52.069
which is what the law requires.

00:57:52.069 --> 00:57:53.360
And it's not just in this case.

00:57:53.360 --> 00:57:54.450
It's in every case.

00:57:54.450 --> 00:57:56.795
So take, say, the bombing
in the Sudan last August.

00:57:56.795 --> 00:58:00.170
I mean, it's now conceded.

00:58:00.170 --> 00:58:03.830
Very few even try to
deny that the US just

00:58:03.830 --> 00:58:06.530
bombed a pharmaceutical
plant in Sudan

00:58:06.530 --> 00:58:10.610
and destroyed half of Sudan's
pharmaceutical industry.

00:58:10.610 --> 00:58:14.010
Well, you know, sometimes
things go wrong.

00:58:14.010 --> 00:58:17.120
There's no talk
about war crimes,

00:58:17.120 --> 00:58:22.880
no talk about reparations, not
even any talk about an apology.

00:58:22.880 --> 00:58:24.620
I mean, why should
we apologize if we

00:58:24.620 --> 00:58:27.230
destroy half of the
pharmaceuticals in a poor East

00:58:27.230 --> 00:58:29.120
African country?

00:58:29.120 --> 00:58:30.530
Let's be serious.

00:58:30.530 --> 00:58:33.590
Furthermore, that's accepted
across the spectrum.

00:58:33.590 --> 00:58:35.030
OK.

00:58:35.030 --> 00:58:37.430
There's a further
history for this.

00:58:37.430 --> 00:58:41.330
10 years ago, the United
States took the same stand,

00:58:41.330 --> 00:58:44.960
explicitly, with regard
to the world court

00:58:44.960 --> 00:58:50.564
dared to follow a case
against the United States

00:58:50.564 --> 00:58:51.980
and, indeed, to
condemn the United

00:58:51.980 --> 00:58:54.550
States for the
unlawful use of force

00:58:54.550 --> 00:58:57.020
in its war against Nicaragua.

00:58:57.020 --> 00:59:01.620
The United States' position
was clear and explicit.

00:59:01.620 --> 00:59:04.100
The State Department, Legal
Department said that we cannot

00:59:04.100 --> 00:59:06.140
accept world court jurisdiction.

00:59:06.140 --> 00:59:07.760
They explained why.

00:59:07.760 --> 00:59:11.122
The reason is that other
states do not agree with us.

00:59:11.122 --> 00:59:12.455
They do not accept our policies.

00:59:12.455 --> 00:59:14.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:14.420 --> 00:59:16.310
And they said we
must, therefore,

00:59:16.310 --> 00:59:19.580
"reserve to ourselves
the right to decide

00:59:19.580 --> 00:59:21.770
when court rulings apply.

00:59:21.770 --> 00:59:25.910
And we must reject court
rulings," I'm quoting now,

00:59:25.910 --> 00:59:29.150
"for any dispute involving
matters essentially

00:59:29.150 --> 00:59:32.210
within the domestic jurisdiction
of the United States,

00:59:32.210 --> 00:59:33.980
as determined by
the United States."

00:59:33.980 --> 00:59:34.880
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:34.880 --> 00:59:38.480
The example in question was
the US war against Nicaragua.

00:59:38.480 --> 00:59:40.250
And the United States
determined that that

00:59:40.250 --> 00:59:42.210
was within its
domestic jurisdiction.

00:59:42.210 --> 00:59:44.510
So the world court
could get lost.

00:59:44.510 --> 00:59:47.060
And that was done
with virtually 100%

00:59:47.060 --> 00:59:50.420
support of educated
intellectuals, including

00:59:50.420 --> 00:59:53.900
leading advocates of world
order who write articles and law

00:59:53.900 --> 00:59:56.280
journals and so on and so forth.

00:59:56.280 --> 00:59:59.600
The New York Times dismissed
the court as a hostile forum.

00:59:59.600 --> 01:00:01.010
And so it went.

01:00:01.010 --> 01:00:03.090
The court judgment was
never even reported.

01:00:03.090 --> 01:00:05.450
And it was radically violated.

01:00:05.450 --> 01:00:07.640
OK, that's the world court.

01:00:07.640 --> 01:00:09.710
This goes back much further.

01:00:09.710 --> 01:00:16.170
Back in 1962, Dean Acheson,
a highly respected statesman,

01:00:16.170 --> 01:00:18.710
senior advisor to the
Kennedy administration,

01:00:18.710 --> 01:00:22.370
informed the American Society
for International Law that

01:00:22.370 --> 01:00:25.280
a situation in which our
country's power position

01:00:25.280 --> 01:00:29.570
and prestige are involved cannot
be treated as a legal issue.

01:00:29.570 --> 01:00:30.530
OK.

01:00:30.530 --> 01:00:32.390
He was referring
to the US embargo

01:00:32.390 --> 01:00:35.690
against Cuba, which, of course,
he recognized was illegal.

01:00:35.690 --> 01:00:39.500
But nothing in which our
power position and prestige

01:00:39.500 --> 01:00:42.470
are involved can be
treated as a legal issue.

01:00:42.470 --> 01:00:44.510
And accordingly,
at that time, you

01:00:44.510 --> 01:00:48.010
heard Adlai Stevenson
at the United Nations

01:00:48.010 --> 01:00:52.090
defending the US attack against
South Vietnam as defense

01:00:52.090 --> 01:00:54.580
against internal aggression.

01:00:54.580 --> 01:00:56.710
The South Vietnamese
were carrying out

01:00:56.710 --> 01:00:59.020
internal aggression
in South Vietnam,

01:00:59.020 --> 01:01:02.110
and we were defending
South Vietnam against them.

01:01:02.110 --> 01:01:04.240
We were defending South
Vietnam against what

01:01:04.240 --> 01:01:07.590
John F. Kennedy called
the "assault from within."

01:01:07.590 --> 01:01:11.130
That was when he launched
the assault from without.

01:01:11.130 --> 01:01:11.830
[LAUGHTER]

01:01:11.830 --> 01:01:13.570
This goes way back.

01:01:13.570 --> 01:01:15.820
In earlier years, it
had been in secret.

01:01:15.820 --> 01:01:20.140
So in earlier years, the
flat rejection of the Charter

01:01:20.140 --> 01:01:21.250
is explicit--

01:01:21.250 --> 01:01:23.250
very explicit-- but in secret.

01:01:23.250 --> 01:01:26.110
It goes back to 1947--

01:01:26.110 --> 01:01:29.920
first memorandum of the
National Security Council,

01:01:29.920 --> 01:01:33.520
in which it called for national
mobilization and the United

01:01:33.520 --> 01:01:38.140
States' support for military
action, paramilitary action,

01:01:38.140 --> 01:01:41.710
other such things in Italy,
if the communists took power

01:01:41.710 --> 01:01:43.584
by legal means in an election.

01:01:43.584 --> 01:01:45.250
In other words, if a
democratic election

01:01:45.250 --> 01:01:49.630
came out the wrong way, we
would use force to overthrow it.

01:01:49.630 --> 01:01:52.810
That's 1947, NSC 1.

01:01:52.810 --> 01:01:55.420
The story goes on from there.

01:01:55.420 --> 01:01:57.460
The innovation in
the Reagan years

01:01:57.460 --> 01:01:59.350
was that the contempt
for international law

01:01:59.350 --> 01:02:01.600
became completely overt.

01:02:01.600 --> 01:02:03.370
It wasn't even secret anymore.

01:02:03.370 --> 01:02:06.190
Under Clinton, it's
lost any pretense.

01:02:06.190 --> 01:02:07.696
So the conclusion is--

01:02:07.696 --> 01:02:09.070
the only reasonable
conclusion is

01:02:09.070 --> 01:02:11.800
that the first pillar
of world order,

01:02:11.800 --> 01:02:15.130
the international political
order, has totally disappeared.

01:02:15.130 --> 01:02:17.650
It's available as a
weapon against enemies,

01:02:17.650 --> 01:02:21.760
but nothing else remains,
except the theological dogma

01:02:21.760 --> 01:02:24.430
that we uphold world order
and international law.

01:02:24.430 --> 01:02:26.980
That remains,
independently of the facts.

01:02:26.980 --> 01:02:29.860
So nothing else remains.

01:02:29.860 --> 01:02:31.855
Well, let's proceed.

01:02:36.280 --> 01:02:38.240
Let's turn to the
Universal Declaration,

01:02:38.240 --> 01:02:40.010
second pillar of world order.

01:02:40.010 --> 01:02:41.855
This is a tightly
integrated document.

01:02:45.440 --> 01:02:47.990
It has conventionally
divided in three parts--

01:02:47.990 --> 01:02:50.810
civil and political rights,
socioeconomic rights, and what

01:02:50.810 --> 01:02:52.850
are called "solidarity rights."

01:02:52.850 --> 01:02:54.410
The integration of
all of those was

01:02:54.410 --> 01:02:56.210
stressed from the beginning.

01:02:56.210 --> 01:02:58.220
You look at the
background, that's clear.

01:02:58.220 --> 01:03:00.140
Repeatedly been stressed since.

01:03:00.140 --> 01:03:04.180
Time's short, so I won't
run through the history.

01:03:04.180 --> 01:03:06.350
The major Law
Review article that

01:03:06.350 --> 01:03:08.300
just appeared on
the 50th anniversary

01:03:08.300 --> 01:03:12.440
by Harvard Law Professor
Mary Ann Glendon

01:03:12.440 --> 01:03:15.800
stresses that the Universal
Declaration elevates

01:03:15.800 --> 01:03:19.010
social, economic, and cultural
rights to fundamental rights

01:03:19.010 --> 01:03:21.590
status and that that
is a crucial part

01:03:21.590 --> 01:03:23.720
of the universalization
of rights carried out

01:03:23.720 --> 01:03:25.790
in the Universal Declaration.

01:03:25.790 --> 01:03:30.110
In short, there is no place for
any sort of relativist demand

01:03:30.110 --> 01:03:35.360
that certain rights must be
relegated to secondary status,

01:03:35.360 --> 01:03:39.680
in accord with, say, Asian
values or some other pretext.

01:03:39.680 --> 01:03:41.960
Glendon also points
out, quite accurately,

01:03:41.960 --> 01:03:47.370
that the support for that was
very broad-based at the time.

01:03:47.370 --> 01:03:48.890
You have to remember the time.

01:03:48.890 --> 01:03:51.920
This is right at the end
of the Second World War.

01:03:51.920 --> 01:03:56.540
There were values of that kind--
the kind expressed in the UD

01:03:56.540 --> 01:04:00.320
were deeply entrenched in the
anti-fascist, popular forces

01:04:00.320 --> 01:04:01.520
in Europe.

01:04:01.520 --> 01:04:04.490
And in the colonial world and
even in the United States,

01:04:04.490 --> 01:04:07.100
that was deeply
disturbing to US elites

01:04:07.100 --> 01:04:11.570
who intended to create a very
different kind of world order

01:04:11.570 --> 01:04:14.330
and, indeed, were quite
explicit about it.

01:04:14.330 --> 01:04:16.610
But the dogma remains.

01:04:16.610 --> 01:04:20.810
And it proclaims a more
self-congratulatory version.

01:04:20.810 --> 01:04:23.930
Well, there were some who
did dismiss the Universal

01:04:23.930 --> 01:04:27.210
Declaration as meaningless.

01:04:27.210 --> 01:04:29.690
So there's an
often quoted phrase

01:04:29.690 --> 01:04:33.080
of Andrey Vyshinsky's,
the Russian delegate

01:04:33.080 --> 01:04:36.140
whose record doesn't
have to detain us.

01:04:36.140 --> 01:04:37.970
He dismissed the
Universal Declaration

01:04:37.970 --> 01:04:41.240
as a "collection
of pious phrases."

01:04:41.240 --> 01:04:43.910
And it was dismissed as,
I'm quoting, "a letter

01:04:43.910 --> 01:04:48.620
to Santa Claus" by Jeane
Kirkpatrick, Reagan's UN

01:04:48.620 --> 01:04:53.300
ambassador, who was deriding
the socioeconomic provisions

01:04:53.300 --> 01:04:55.370
of the Universal Declaration.

01:04:55.370 --> 01:04:57.290
Moving on a couple
of years, it is

01:04:57.290 --> 01:05:01.970
an "empty vessel," a "dangerous
incitement," "preposterous."

01:05:01.970 --> 01:05:05.420
That's Morris Abram,
UN ambassador to the UN

01:05:05.420 --> 01:05:07.740
Commission on Human Rights.

01:05:07.740 --> 01:05:12.110
He's explaining
Washington's rejection

01:05:12.110 --> 01:05:14.450
of what was called the
right to development, which

01:05:14.450 --> 01:05:17.090
if you look at it, is
a very close paraphrase

01:05:17.090 --> 01:05:22.180
of the socioeconomic provisions
of the Universal Declaration.

01:05:22.180 --> 01:05:25.340
That was vetoed by
the United States,

01:05:25.340 --> 01:05:30.785
alone, essentially vetoing
the socioeconomic provisions

01:05:30.785 --> 01:05:32.180
of the UD.

01:05:32.180 --> 01:05:36.230
The US also radically violates
the civil and political

01:05:36.230 --> 01:05:37.910
provisions.

01:05:37.910 --> 01:05:41.210
I won't go through the
details, but quite clear

01:05:41.210 --> 01:05:42.170
and not surprising.

01:05:42.170 --> 01:05:44.540
One of the latest major
Amnesty International reports

01:05:44.540 --> 01:05:46.460
is about this.

01:05:46.460 --> 01:05:48.650
In brief, two pillars
of world order

01:05:48.650 --> 01:05:54.290
have been effectively demolished
except as ideological weapons.

01:05:54.290 --> 01:05:56.300
That doesn't mean that
the people of the world

01:05:56.300 --> 01:05:57.890
shouldn't defend them.

01:05:57.890 --> 01:06:00.830
They do remain
ideals that people

01:06:00.830 --> 01:06:03.650
should try to defend,
they should fight for,

01:06:03.650 --> 01:06:05.330
and that they should
also transcend,

01:06:05.330 --> 01:06:08.240
because they're not the end
of the road by any means.

01:06:08.240 --> 01:06:10.520
But to take that stand
in the United States,

01:06:10.520 --> 01:06:14.880
you have to first carry out an
act of intellectual liberation.

01:06:14.880 --> 01:06:18.710
You have to free yourselves
from the doctrines of the faith.

01:06:18.710 --> 01:06:22.130
You don't do that, you can't
even enter into the fray.

01:06:22.130 --> 01:06:25.610
First have to free yourselves
from the doctrines of the faith

01:06:25.610 --> 01:06:27.800
and at least be
willing to subject them

01:06:27.800 --> 01:06:29.990
to empirical verification.

01:06:29.990 --> 01:06:32.480
If they're not even subject
to empirical verification,

01:06:32.480 --> 01:06:36.040
there's no point discussing
these matters, clearly.

01:06:36.040 --> 01:06:38.970
If they are, you can ask
whether this analysis is correct

01:06:38.970 --> 01:06:41.350
or some different
analysis is correct.

01:06:41.350 --> 01:06:44.470
Well, just the willingness
to subject them

01:06:44.470 --> 01:06:46.900
to examination, the
doctrines, that's

01:06:46.900 --> 01:06:51.790
a radical departure from
prevailing norms, including

01:06:51.790 --> 01:06:54.360
everything in the educational
system and academic scholarship

01:06:54.360 --> 01:06:56.600
and the media and
everywhere else.

01:06:56.600 --> 01:06:57.970
So it's not small.

01:06:57.970 --> 01:07:00.850
Well, couple of words about the
third pillar, the Bretton Woods

01:07:00.850 --> 01:07:06.370
System, which, again, was
totally tightly integrated

01:07:06.370 --> 01:07:07.760
with the others.

01:07:07.760 --> 01:07:10.120
And that's worth understanding.

01:07:10.120 --> 01:07:12.160
A central component of
the Bretton Woods System

01:07:12.160 --> 01:07:15.430
was regulation of finance.

01:07:15.430 --> 01:07:18.620
And there were several
reasons for this.

01:07:18.620 --> 01:07:21.770
One reason was that
it was understood

01:07:21.770 --> 01:07:25.390
that deregulation of finance
would harm the global economy.

01:07:25.390 --> 01:07:27.200
There are good reasons for that.

01:07:27.200 --> 01:07:30.250
And they've been
illustrated in recent years.

01:07:30.250 --> 01:07:33.220
The trouble is that financial
markets, as was understood,

01:07:33.220 --> 01:07:36.400
are quite volatile
and unpredictable.

01:07:36.400 --> 01:07:39.310
To quote a couple
of MIT economists,

01:07:39.310 --> 01:07:43.060
specialists on this, "Financial
markets are governed by panics,

01:07:43.060 --> 01:07:45.850
manias, and crashes.

01:07:45.850 --> 01:07:48.460
They swing far above
and below any kind

01:07:48.460 --> 01:07:50.680
of sensible, fundamental
values, creating

01:07:50.680 --> 01:07:52.990
bubbles that will burst."

01:07:52.990 --> 01:07:55.270
Paul Samuelson, who says
the evidence for that

01:07:55.270 --> 01:07:56.900
is overwhelming.

01:07:56.900 --> 01:07:58.990
And this was understood
back in the '40s.

01:07:58.990 --> 01:08:01.270
So you deregulate
financial markets,

01:08:01.270 --> 01:08:03.130
you're going to have a disaster.

01:08:03.130 --> 01:08:05.530
And it'll harm the
general economy.

01:08:05.530 --> 01:08:07.020
That was one reason.

01:08:07.020 --> 01:08:09.790
A second reason
was closely related

01:08:09.790 --> 01:08:12.890
to world order and human rights.

01:08:12.890 --> 01:08:16.810
One reason for both capital
controls and keeping currencies

01:08:16.810 --> 01:08:22.450
within a narrow band was
that it was understood

01:08:22.450 --> 01:08:25.210
that deregulation of capital
is a tremendous weapon

01:08:25.210 --> 01:08:29.330
against democracy and
socioeconomic rights.

01:08:29.330 --> 01:08:31.300
If we're mentioning
that, let me just

01:08:31.300 --> 01:08:33.550
say that the current
debate between the United

01:08:33.550 --> 01:08:38.020
States and its G7 partners
is over keeping currencies

01:08:38.020 --> 01:08:40.090
within a narrow band.

01:08:40.090 --> 01:08:42.930
If you read the
articles on this,

01:08:42.930 --> 01:08:46.960
you'll notice that
the G7 position--

01:08:46.960 --> 01:08:49.569
Germany, Japan,
France, and so on--

01:08:49.569 --> 01:08:51.859
maybe not Britain, but
the other G7 countries--

01:08:51.859 --> 01:08:54.970
is that the major
currencies should

01:08:54.970 --> 01:08:59.140
be kept sort of closely
interconnected, [? not ?]

01:08:59.140 --> 01:09:01.210
too volatile.

01:09:01.210 --> 01:09:05.529
And that was rejected as crazy
by US Secretary of the Treasury

01:09:05.529 --> 01:09:09.340
Robert Rubin, because if
we were to agree to that,

01:09:09.340 --> 01:09:13.569
it would mean that some other
authority might determine

01:09:13.569 --> 01:09:15.640
that we're not allowed
to lower interest rates

01:09:15.640 --> 01:09:18.010
to stimulate the
economy if we want to.

01:09:18.010 --> 01:09:20.120
So if the economy's
sinking into recession,

01:09:20.120 --> 01:09:22.540
we won't be able to allow
interest rates because that'll

01:09:22.540 --> 01:09:24.430
affect currency values.

01:09:24.430 --> 01:09:27.790
And we'd be crazy to
allow any other country,

01:09:27.790 --> 01:09:31.870
any international organization
to prevent us from carrying out

01:09:31.870 --> 01:09:33.430
those fiscal policies--

01:09:33.430 --> 01:09:35.620
exactly the fiscal
policies that we

01:09:35.620 --> 01:09:39.100
force on every other country,
as the very same articles

01:09:39.100 --> 01:09:43.220
point out, without any
sense of contradiction.

01:09:43.220 --> 01:09:46.060
Take a look, and think about it.

01:09:46.060 --> 01:09:49.750
Why is deregulation of finance
a weapon against democracy

01:09:49.750 --> 01:09:51.279
and human rights?

01:09:51.279 --> 01:09:54.550
Well, very obviously,
and it was understood.

01:09:54.550 --> 01:10:01.180
The reason is that if
capital flow is unregulated,

01:10:01.180 --> 01:10:04.540
it will quickly create what
some internationalist economists

01:10:04.540 --> 01:10:06.770
call a virtual senate--

01:10:06.770 --> 01:10:09.520
a senate consisting of
concentrated international

01:10:09.520 --> 01:10:11.590
financial capital,
which will simply

01:10:11.590 --> 01:10:16.060
impose decisions on governments
by the threat of capital

01:10:16.060 --> 01:10:17.120
flight.

01:10:17.120 --> 01:10:21.100
So if some government
pursues irrational policies,

01:10:21.100 --> 01:10:24.170
so policies that are aimed
at helping the population,

01:10:24.170 --> 01:10:27.160
let's say, instead of raising
profits for investors and are

01:10:27.160 --> 01:10:30.400
therefore irrational--
like education or health

01:10:30.400 --> 01:10:33.460
or environmental policies
or whatever it may be--

01:10:33.460 --> 01:10:35.290
you can force them
to stop, simply

01:10:35.290 --> 01:10:40.660
by pulling all the capital
out of the country, which

01:10:40.660 --> 01:10:43.630
forces interest rates up, sends
the country to a depression,

01:10:43.630 --> 01:10:46.340
and all the usual consequences.

01:10:46.340 --> 01:10:50.130
So therefore, unless
capital is controlled,

01:10:50.130 --> 01:10:54.280
you've very sharply restricted
a democracy and human rights.

01:10:54.280 --> 01:10:58.900
Well, that was understood
then, and it is understood now.

01:10:58.900 --> 01:11:01.870
The Bretton Woods System,
with financial regulation,

01:11:01.870 --> 01:11:05.960
persisted through roughly 1970.

01:11:05.960 --> 01:11:08.770
That's a period that's
commonly called the Golden

01:11:08.770 --> 01:11:13.250
Age of Post-War Capitalism,
a period of high growth,

01:11:13.250 --> 01:11:15.820
high growth of
productivity, expansion

01:11:15.820 --> 01:11:19.040
of the social contract, and
the so-called welfare state,

01:11:19.040 --> 01:11:21.970
which turned the Universal
Declaration into something

01:11:21.970 --> 01:11:24.730
a little more than a
"letter to Santa Claus."

01:11:24.730 --> 01:11:28.750
From the early 1970s, that
system was dismantled--

01:11:28.750 --> 01:11:30.700
first at the initiative
of the United States,

01:11:30.700 --> 01:11:33.156
Britain came along,
later, others--

01:11:33.156 --> 01:11:36.750
and other major economies,
not until the '80s.

01:11:36.750 --> 01:11:39.930
And the results wouldn't
have surprised the founders

01:11:39.930 --> 01:11:42.070
of the Bretton Woods System.

01:11:42.070 --> 01:11:44.390
The results have transferred--

01:11:44.390 --> 01:11:47.970
well, some economists call it
a transfer from a golden age

01:11:47.970 --> 01:11:49.860
to a leaden age.

01:11:49.860 --> 01:11:54.390
In the period since, there has
been a slowdown of the economy,

01:11:54.390 --> 01:11:56.070
globally.

01:11:56.070 --> 01:11:59.460
And the social contract has
been dissolved dramatically

01:11:59.460 --> 01:12:02.340
in the US and Britain,
to some extent elsewhere.

01:12:02.340 --> 01:12:03.960
Inequality has gone way up.

01:12:06.565 --> 01:12:10.470
The consequences, I have
already described briefly.

01:12:10.470 --> 01:12:13.380
One region of the world
temporarily escaped from this.

01:12:13.380 --> 01:12:16.860
That's East Asia, which is quite
different from Southeast Asia.

01:12:16.860 --> 01:12:18.165
There, there was--

01:12:18.165 --> 01:12:20.610
I'll quote the chief
economist of the World Bank,

01:12:20.610 --> 01:12:23.700
there was "an unprecedented
economic miracle,"

01:12:23.700 --> 01:12:25.410
which was based on
the fact that they

01:12:25.410 --> 01:12:28.260
disregarded "the
religion," as he calls it--

01:12:28.260 --> 01:12:30.630
"the religion that
markets know best."

01:12:30.630 --> 01:12:32.447
And they disregarded
the prescriptions

01:12:32.447 --> 01:12:34.280
of the international
financial institutions,

01:12:34.280 --> 01:12:36.750
the so-called
Washington Consensus.

01:12:36.750 --> 01:12:41.130
And they carried out an
unprecedented economic miracle.

01:12:41.130 --> 01:12:47.160
In the early 1990s, South
Korea liberalized capital flow

01:12:47.160 --> 01:12:49.170
under very heavy US
pressure, I should

01:12:49.170 --> 01:12:51.150
say-- one of several factors.

01:12:51.150 --> 01:12:53.950
That's a large factor in
the subsequent collapse.

01:12:53.950 --> 01:12:57.750
That's generally agreed.

01:12:57.750 --> 01:13:03.270
Up till 1997 or 1998, the
global economic system

01:13:03.270 --> 01:13:05.810
was considered really great.

01:13:05.810 --> 01:13:09.490
It was, in fact, what's called
an economic miracle, fairy

01:13:09.490 --> 01:13:13.530
tale, very much
the way Mexico was

01:13:13.530 --> 01:13:15.690
an economic miracle
during the period when

01:13:15.690 --> 01:13:18.720
the number of billionaires
was rising about as

01:13:18.720 --> 01:13:21.450
fast as the poverty level.

01:13:21.450 --> 01:13:25.590
There was no crisis
until 1997, '98.

01:13:25.590 --> 01:13:28.500
All there was anguish
and fear, insecurity

01:13:28.500 --> 01:13:30.810
about jobs, and a life
of quiet desperation,

01:13:30.810 --> 01:13:33.240
to quote the head of UNCTAD.

01:13:33.240 --> 01:13:35.010
But that's not a crisis.

01:13:35.010 --> 01:13:38.100
That's a miracle.

01:13:38.100 --> 01:13:40.710
Because that was only
outside the doors, remember.

01:13:40.710 --> 01:13:45.600
Inside, the festive
atmosphere was exuberant.

01:13:45.600 --> 01:13:49.710
So but by 1998, the
festive atmosphere

01:13:49.710 --> 01:13:54.240
inside was becoming
a little disturbed.

01:13:54.240 --> 01:13:57.180
The interests of rich
people were being affected.

01:13:57.180 --> 01:14:01.330
So we moved from economic
miracle to crisis.

01:14:01.330 --> 01:14:02.716
Those are technical terms.

01:14:02.716 --> 01:14:04.590
Again, it doesn't have
much to do with what's

01:14:04.590 --> 01:14:05.589
happening of the people.

01:14:05.589 --> 01:14:08.440
It has to do a lot with what's
happening to rich people--

01:14:08.440 --> 01:14:10.052
rich, powerful people.

01:14:10.052 --> 01:14:12.135
And their interests were
starting to be disturbed.

01:14:12.135 --> 01:14:14.115
So all of a sudden,
we had a crisis.

01:14:14.115 --> 01:14:17.700
And that's distinct from
anguish and fear and so on.

01:14:17.700 --> 01:14:21.785
Well, that crisis is now
all over the front pages.

01:14:21.785 --> 01:14:25.800
So I won't waste any time on
it, though it's interesting.

01:14:25.800 --> 01:14:29.760
One side effect of the crisis
is that all of the certainties

01:14:29.760 --> 01:14:31.630
have dissolved.

01:14:31.630 --> 01:14:34.830
So the Bank for
International Settlements

01:14:34.830 --> 01:14:38.580
in Basel, which is the most
conservative and respectable

01:14:38.580 --> 01:14:40.210
institution that exists.

01:14:40.210 --> 01:14:43.230
It's called the "central
bank of central bankers."

01:14:43.230 --> 01:14:45.390
On their latest
report last summer,

01:14:45.390 --> 01:14:49.500
they said nobody had a
clue as to what's going on.

01:14:49.500 --> 01:14:52.170
And they urged that
we have humility.

01:14:52.170 --> 01:14:55.470
Instead of issuing
confident pronouncements,

01:14:55.470 --> 01:14:57.510
let's at least have
enough humility

01:14:57.510 --> 01:15:00.150
to admit that we haven't got
a clue as to what is happening

01:15:00.150 --> 01:15:01.920
or what to do about it.

01:15:01.920 --> 01:15:05.160
Leading economists from
around here, Cambridge,

01:15:05.160 --> 01:15:08.940
have been recently publishing
articles in which they say that

01:15:08.940 --> 01:15:13.000
the international economy
is dimly understood,

01:15:13.000 --> 01:15:16.200
that we may be heading
into a third-world-style,

01:15:16.200 --> 01:15:19.200
'30s-style depression and we
don't know what to do about it.

01:15:19.200 --> 01:15:22.290
Actually, the real crisis
is much more fundamental.

01:15:22.290 --> 01:15:27.990
And I'll finish by just giving
two quotes that I think capture

01:15:27.990 --> 01:15:32.520
it rather well, both recent.

01:15:32.520 --> 01:15:35.760
One is from an event
that was organized

01:15:35.760 --> 01:15:42.100
by Jesse Jackson over Martin
Luther King weekend in January.

01:15:42.100 --> 01:15:45.510
This was an event in
Manhattan to support

01:15:45.510 --> 01:15:50.640
Bill Clinton in the moment
of his terrible trials.

01:15:50.640 --> 01:15:53.760
There were lots of celebrities
who came and sort of admired

01:15:53.760 --> 01:15:54.870
one another and so on.

01:15:54.870 --> 01:15:55.680
[LAUGHTER]

01:15:55.680 --> 01:16:00.260
Among them was the president
of the New York Stock Exchange.

01:16:00.260 --> 01:16:02.510
I'm quoting from the press now.

01:16:02.510 --> 01:16:05.400
New York Times, "He
told Mr. Clinton

01:16:05.400 --> 01:16:09.480
that Dr. King is surely
smiling down on the gathering,

01:16:09.480 --> 01:16:11.880
recognizing how
Clinton had benefited

01:16:11.880 --> 01:16:15.210
my little corner of
southern Manhattan," which

01:16:15.210 --> 01:16:17.100
is quite accurate.

01:16:17.100 --> 01:16:19.470
Other little corners
of southern Manhattan

01:16:19.470 --> 01:16:25.830
fared rather differently, but
the stock exchange, on that,

01:16:25.830 --> 01:16:28.600
Mr. Clinton doubtless
showered many benefits.

01:16:28.600 --> 01:16:31.460
So no doubt, Martin Luther
King is smiling down on him.

01:16:31.460 --> 01:16:32.280
[LAUGHTER]

01:16:33.100 --> 01:16:37.660
That's an appropriate comment
reflecting the realities

01:16:37.660 --> 01:16:41.350
of a political system in
which, in the last election,

01:16:41.350 --> 01:16:45.250
November, 1998--

01:16:45.250 --> 01:16:47.110
data have just
come in recently--

01:16:47.110 --> 01:16:49.540
95% percent of
winning candidates

01:16:49.540 --> 01:16:51.670
outspent their
opponents, meaning

01:16:51.670 --> 01:16:55.570
you could predict up
to 95% accuracy who's

01:16:55.570 --> 01:16:58.534
going to win just looking
at how much money they had.

01:16:58.534 --> 01:16:59.950
So it's not like
Russia, where you

01:16:59.950 --> 01:17:02.029
could predict 100%
that it was going

01:17:02.029 --> 01:17:03.112
to be the Communist Party.

01:17:03.112 --> 01:17:03.611
[LAUGHTER]

01:17:03.611 --> 01:17:05.770
It's only 95%.

01:17:05.770 --> 01:17:09.640
Furthermore, if you look
at the contributions--

01:17:09.640 --> 01:17:13.090
same data-- business
contributions

01:17:13.090 --> 01:17:16.600
outspent labor contributions
by about 12 to 1,

01:17:16.600 --> 01:17:18.400
which is highly
misleading, remember,

01:17:18.400 --> 01:17:22.030
because labor represents
way more people.

01:17:22.030 --> 01:17:25.360
And individual contributions,
though they're not measured,

01:17:25.360 --> 01:17:28.310
undoubtedly are skewed
at least as much.

01:17:28.310 --> 01:17:32.890
So what that translates
into, to put it into English,

01:17:32.890 --> 01:17:37.420
is that a little corner
of southern Manhattan

01:17:37.420 --> 01:17:39.070
and a couple of
other little corners

01:17:39.070 --> 01:17:43.900
like it, essentially purchased
candidates and put them

01:17:43.900 --> 01:17:46.150
in office and then set
conditions that they're

01:17:46.150 --> 01:17:48.280
going to have to meet or else.

01:17:48.280 --> 01:17:50.710
And also set the general
framework for policy, just

01:17:50.710 --> 01:17:53.960
by virtue of their power--

01:17:53.960 --> 01:17:56.540
becoming the virtual
senate, as I mentioned,

01:17:56.540 --> 01:17:59.110
once these instruments
are in their hands.

01:18:01.960 --> 01:18:05.410
Of course, they also
require a powerful state.

01:18:05.410 --> 01:18:07.090
They're very insistent on that.

01:18:07.090 --> 01:18:09.700
There has to be a
powerful state, which

01:18:09.700 --> 01:18:13.270
will socialize risks
and costs and we'll

01:18:13.270 --> 01:18:16.780
make sure that there's no
unpleasant noises in servants'

01:18:16.780 --> 01:18:17.450
quarters.

01:18:17.450 --> 01:18:21.350
So that's still got to be there,
but not with its old functions.

01:18:21.350 --> 01:18:24.790
Second quote, last one, is from
David Rockefeller, reflecting

01:18:24.790 --> 01:18:26.590
on the current scene.

01:18:26.590 --> 01:18:30.160
Recall that David Rockefeller
is at the liberal end

01:18:30.160 --> 01:18:30.920
of the spectrum.

01:18:30.920 --> 01:18:35.500
He's part of the "Establishment
Left," it's called--

01:18:35.500 --> 01:18:38.040
without irony, I should say.

01:18:38.040 --> 01:18:41.220
He's commenting on the reduction
of the role of government

01:18:41.220 --> 01:18:44.830
in public affairs, something
that business has always

01:18:44.830 --> 01:18:47.080
favored, he says.

01:18:47.080 --> 01:18:49.180
Side comment-- the
role of government

01:18:49.180 --> 01:18:52.270
means the role of the
population, right?

01:18:52.270 --> 01:18:55.690
Of all institutions that are
around, with all its flaws,

01:18:55.690 --> 01:18:58.570
whatever they may be,
government is the only one

01:18:58.570 --> 01:19:00.250
in which, at least
in principle, there's

01:19:00.250 --> 01:19:02.950
some possibility
of participation--

01:19:02.950 --> 01:19:04.930
often in practice, too.

01:19:04.930 --> 01:19:07.270
In the operations
of General Electric,

01:19:07.270 --> 01:19:10.570
there are no possibilities
in principle.

01:19:10.570 --> 01:19:13.240
So government is
the one institution

01:19:13.240 --> 01:19:16.630
of the general institutional
framework in which people

01:19:16.630 --> 01:19:19.330
may have a role, sometimes do.

01:19:19.330 --> 01:19:23.020
And that role is declining.

01:19:23.020 --> 01:19:25.420
Business is happy about that.

01:19:25.420 --> 01:19:28.090
But he says, "While
that reduction

01:19:28.090 --> 01:19:32.000
of democratic participation
is, of course, welcome,

01:19:32.000 --> 01:19:34.420
there is another
side to that coin.

01:19:34.420 --> 01:19:37.630
Somebody has to take
government's place.

01:19:37.630 --> 01:19:41.660
And business seems to be the
logical entity to do it."

01:19:41.660 --> 01:19:43.870
OK, that's Rockefeller.

01:19:43.870 --> 01:19:46.150
He goes on to say it's the
responsibility of business

01:19:46.150 --> 01:19:48.640
to fill this gap made
by the disappearance

01:19:48.640 --> 01:19:50.470
of democratic government.

01:19:50.470 --> 01:19:53.680
It's certainly not the
role of the public.

01:19:53.680 --> 01:19:54.850
That's for sure.

01:19:54.850 --> 01:19:57.250
That's the Establishment Left.

01:19:57.250 --> 01:20:01.510
When you move over to the Right,
the message gets a lot harsher.

01:20:01.510 --> 01:20:03.580
Well, that's a
pretty fair picture

01:20:03.580 --> 01:20:06.850
of the current drift of policy.

01:20:06.850 --> 01:20:09.760
It's true, you know corporations
gained the right of persons

01:20:09.760 --> 01:20:13.240
through judicial activism
early in this century.

01:20:13.240 --> 01:20:16.190
They're not ordinary
persons, like you and me.

01:20:16.190 --> 01:20:17.500
They are immortal persons.

01:20:17.500 --> 01:20:22.660
They have extraordinary power,
unlike flesh and blood persons.

01:20:22.660 --> 01:20:26.260
They also demand the rights
of states, and they get them.

01:20:26.260 --> 01:20:28.180
Under NAFTA, they've
partially gained them.

01:20:28.180 --> 01:20:31.180
Under the MAI, they'd gain
them still more fully.

01:20:31.180 --> 01:20:33.850
In fact, they would
gain additional rights,

01:20:33.850 --> 01:20:36.160
which are described as
if they're innocuous.

01:20:36.160 --> 01:20:38.410
But they surely aren't
if you think about them.

01:20:38.410 --> 01:20:41.470
Under MAI, these "persons"--

01:20:41.470 --> 01:20:44.350
fictitious, legal persons--
are demanding what

01:20:44.350 --> 01:20:46.270
they call national treatment.

01:20:46.270 --> 01:20:49.990
That's a right that no flesh
and blood person can claim.

01:20:49.990 --> 01:20:53.410
So under national
treatment, General Electric

01:20:53.410 --> 01:20:56.350
can function freely in Mexico.

01:20:56.350 --> 01:20:58.510
But suppose that
some flesh and blood

01:20:58.510 --> 01:21:02.010
Mexican tries to get national
treatment in New York.

01:21:02.010 --> 01:21:04.070
No, that's not going to work.

01:21:04.070 --> 01:21:08.590
So this is only for fictitious,
collectivist, legal entities.

01:21:08.590 --> 01:21:10.270
Those kind of persons
get the rights

01:21:10.270 --> 01:21:15.190
of states, the rights
of national treatment.

01:21:15.190 --> 01:21:18.070
And furthermore, they have
a right and a responsibility

01:21:18.070 --> 01:21:19.810
to take over the
functions of government.

01:21:19.810 --> 01:21:21.550
Now, they must do that.

01:21:21.550 --> 01:21:25.820
That would, therefore, reduce
democratic form still further.

01:21:25.820 --> 01:21:28.120
Internally, they're
basically tyrannies.

01:21:28.120 --> 01:21:30.730
I mean, I don't think
that's in question.

01:21:30.730 --> 01:21:35.220
And they try to run the
global society, including

01:21:35.220 --> 01:21:38.460
the sort of minimal
market system,

01:21:38.460 --> 01:21:40.770
in an integrated fashion.

01:21:40.770 --> 01:21:43.140
They do rely on
the powerful states

01:21:43.140 --> 01:21:46.210
to make sure that they
can get what they want.

01:21:46.210 --> 01:21:48.210
They also demand
and, in large part,

01:21:48.210 --> 01:21:52.770
gain the right to shape opinions
and attitudes and beliefs.

01:21:52.770 --> 01:21:55.410
That's the role of
the corporate media--

01:21:55.410 --> 01:21:59.040
and in fact, the educational
system to a large extent--

01:21:59.040 --> 01:22:02.250
that is, if the right to
define what a human being is,

01:22:02.250 --> 01:22:06.240
what constitutes a
human life, and do so.

01:22:06.240 --> 01:22:09.450
Well, quite apart from
the continuing assault

01:22:09.450 --> 01:22:14.250
against the proclaimed values
of democracy and human rights

01:22:14.250 --> 01:22:19.470
and freedom and all good
things, these tendencies,

01:22:19.470 --> 01:22:22.020
if they are
tolerated, could lead

01:22:22.020 --> 01:22:26.820
to quite serious and possibly
even terminal catastrophes.

01:22:26.820 --> 01:22:29.940
That's only a
speculation, of course.

01:22:29.940 --> 01:22:32.880
Your speculations are
as good as anybody's.

01:22:32.880 --> 01:22:36.120
What is not a speculation
is that the tendencies

01:22:36.120 --> 01:22:38.430
don't have to be tolerated.

01:22:38.430 --> 01:22:39.900
That's a choice.

01:22:39.900 --> 01:22:42.000
You can make it and not make it.

01:22:42.000 --> 01:22:44.500
It's not a necessity.

01:22:44.500 --> 01:22:47.820
The ability to make
that choice is roughly

01:22:47.820 --> 01:22:50.955
measured by your
share in privilege.

01:22:50.955 --> 01:22:53.760
The more access to privilege
you have, the more of a choice

01:22:53.760 --> 01:22:54.790
you can make.

01:22:54.790 --> 01:22:58.590
It's also measured by the
freedom of the society.

01:22:58.590 --> 01:23:03.120
That means that for people like
us, the options are wide open.

01:23:03.120 --> 01:23:06.060
And one thing we can be
reasonably confident of,

01:23:06.060 --> 01:23:10.380
I think, is that neither
conscience nor history

01:23:10.380 --> 01:23:13.350
is going to look favorably
upon an unwillingness

01:23:13.350 --> 01:23:17.781
to face those decisions
with care and dedication.

01:23:17.781 --> 01:23:18.775
Thanks.

01:23:18.775 --> 01:23:22.254
[APPLAUSE]

01:23:50.110 --> 01:23:51.570
Are there any mics?

01:23:51.570 --> 01:23:52.687
Are there any mics?

01:23:52.687 --> 01:23:54.270
JANE GOULD: Probably
not in the house.

01:23:54.270 --> 01:23:55.561
NOAM CHOMSKY: Not in the house.

01:23:55.561 --> 01:23:57.190
OK, if people want
to say something,

01:23:57.190 --> 01:24:00.330
maybe stand up so as to
maximize the chance somebody

01:24:00.330 --> 01:24:01.115
will hear you.

01:24:01.115 --> 01:24:02.005
Go ahead.

01:24:02.005 --> 01:24:03.340
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:24:03.340 --> 01:24:05.180
NOAM CHOMSKY: Well, OK.

01:24:05.180 --> 01:24:05.980
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

01:24:05.980 --> 01:24:06.760
NOAM CHOMSKY: You guys fight.

01:24:06.760 --> 01:24:07.260
Good.

01:24:07.260 --> 01:24:10.930
AUDIENCE: Some of us--

01:24:10.930 --> 01:24:13.820
part of the educational
system as you've

01:24:13.820 --> 01:24:16.430
described, not so
much in detail,

01:24:16.430 --> 01:24:19.560
right here in
Massachusetts, John Silber,

01:24:19.560 --> 01:24:22.240
someone with whom I believe
you may have crossed swords,

01:24:22.240 --> 01:24:26.230
is leading the war
against public education.

01:24:26.230 --> 01:24:31.000
And he and his colleagues are
using a variety of tactics.

01:24:31.000 --> 01:24:34.420
There is a [? laying ?] bill
before the Massachusetts

01:24:34.420 --> 01:24:38.020
Congress, which will take
away collective bargaining

01:24:38.020 --> 01:24:43.395
rights for teachers-- in effect,
completely gut the unions.

01:24:43.395 --> 01:24:46.960
The point is that some of
us in the teachers unions

01:24:46.960 --> 01:24:50.260
are going to confront Silber.

01:24:50.260 --> 01:24:53.330
And it occurred to me
that, if I'm not mistaken,

01:24:53.330 --> 01:24:56.440
he was sending a
Kissinger commission

01:24:56.440 --> 01:25:03.100
that purported to investigate
American policy in Latin

01:25:03.100 --> 01:25:03.762
America.

01:25:03.762 --> 01:25:04.720
NOAM CHOMSKY: Could be.

01:25:04.720 --> 01:25:05.290
I don't remember.

01:25:05.290 --> 01:25:05.998
AUDIENCE: Oh, OK.

01:25:08.420 --> 01:25:10.302
Let me put it another way, then.

01:25:10.302 --> 01:25:12.260
It was my recollection
that [? were ?] [? at ?]

01:25:12.260 --> 01:25:14.320
[? crossed ?] swords with
him at one time or another.

01:25:14.320 --> 01:25:15.020
NOAM CHOMSKY: Oh, yeah.

01:25:15.020 --> 01:25:15.370
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:25:15.370 --> 01:25:17.578
NOAM CHOMSKY: I'm not one
of his favorite characters.

01:25:17.578 --> 01:25:20.144
AUDIENCE: [? Quite. ?] And
of course, Professor Zinn.

01:25:20.144 --> 01:25:21.810
I know some of the
history between them.

01:25:21.810 --> 01:25:22.690
NOAM CHOMSKY: Yeah.

01:25:22.690 --> 01:25:24.856
AUDIENCE: I was wondering
if you had anything to say

01:25:24.856 --> 01:25:27.122
about a person who would--

01:25:27.122 --> 01:25:29.330
NOAM CHOMSKY: About him,
you make your own decisions.

01:25:29.330 --> 01:25:30.247
I don't-- excuse me.

01:25:30.247 --> 01:25:31.080
AUDIENCE: All right.

01:25:31.080 --> 01:25:32.440
NOAM CHOMSKY: Should
I repeat-- yeah.

01:25:32.440 --> 01:25:33.400
Let me repeat the question.

01:25:33.400 --> 01:25:35.470
I mean, the core of the
question-- the comment,

01:25:35.470 --> 01:25:38.180
really-- is that
John Silber, who's--

01:25:38.180 --> 01:25:38.680
what is he?

01:25:38.680 --> 01:25:41.710
Chancellor of something or the
state system or something--

01:25:41.710 --> 01:25:45.900
is leading a campaign now to
try to undermine the Teachers

01:25:45.900 --> 01:25:49.210
Union, which is part
of a general attack

01:25:49.210 --> 01:25:51.760
on the public education system.

01:25:51.760 --> 01:25:52.960
Is that right?

01:25:52.960 --> 01:25:56.830
Then come some questions about
our personal relations, which--

01:25:56.830 --> 01:26:02.330
put to the side, because
this is a general.

01:26:02.330 --> 01:26:05.920
There's I think a serious
issue-- very serious.

01:26:05.920 --> 01:26:11.890
One of the areas of government,
meaning public participation

01:26:11.890 --> 01:26:14.710
that still exists
is the schools.

01:26:14.710 --> 01:26:16.920
And at the left end
of the spectrum,

01:26:16.920 --> 01:26:19.870
people like David Rockefeller
would very much like

01:26:19.870 --> 01:26:24.640
to get rid of this government
intervention in the economy,

01:26:24.640 --> 01:26:30.430
or as I'm told that the latest
edition of Paul Samuelson's

01:26:30.430 --> 01:26:32.410
Economics changes the
phrase to government

01:26:32.410 --> 01:26:34.840
"interference" in the economy.

01:26:34.840 --> 01:26:35.890
Tell me me if I'm right.

01:26:35.890 --> 01:26:40.630
That would then introduce an
important doctrinal assumption

01:26:40.630 --> 01:26:43.840
into the description.

01:26:43.840 --> 01:26:45.850
So we want to get rid of
government interference

01:26:45.850 --> 01:26:48.310
in the economy like
public schools, which

01:26:48.310 --> 01:26:50.800
have all sorts of bad
features-- like they cultivate

01:26:50.800 --> 01:26:54.230
a sense of solidarity or
of care for other people.

01:26:54.230 --> 01:26:56.740
Like if there's a public
school system, that's

01:26:56.740 --> 01:26:59.260
an expression of the fact that
you care whether the kid down

01:26:59.260 --> 01:27:01.200
the street gets an education.

01:27:01.200 --> 01:27:04.690
And that's a very bad thing
because you're supposed to be--

01:27:04.690 --> 01:27:06.730
you get this
message from infancy

01:27:06.730 --> 01:27:10.450
on through the television
set and everything else

01:27:10.450 --> 01:27:12.910
that the only value
of a human life

01:27:12.910 --> 01:27:16.390
is to maximize what the
advertising industry calls

01:27:16.390 --> 01:27:18.190
"invented wants."

01:27:18.190 --> 01:27:20.024
So they're supposed to
invent wants for you.

01:27:20.024 --> 01:27:21.564
And you're supposed
to maximize them.

01:27:21.564 --> 01:27:23.950
And that's the only thing
you're supposed to care about--

01:27:23.950 --> 01:27:28.810
not care about anybody else,
not care about control your life

01:27:28.810 --> 01:27:29.320
and work.

01:27:29.320 --> 01:27:31.300
That's out of the door.

01:27:31.300 --> 01:27:35.140
But maximize your
own fabricated wants.

01:27:35.140 --> 01:27:38.950
And the existence of
the public school system

01:27:38.950 --> 01:27:41.620
is inconsistent with
that, because it's

01:27:41.620 --> 01:27:46.500
an expression of solidarity and
care for others and compassion

01:27:46.500 --> 01:27:49.060
and all sorts of
ideals that are not

01:27:49.060 --> 01:27:52.610
supposed to exist in the
system that is being created--

01:27:52.610 --> 01:27:55.920
the ideological system
it's being created.

01:27:55.920 --> 01:27:57.640
It's not only John Silber.

01:27:57.640 --> 01:28:01.130
There's a major assault across
the boards on public schools.

01:28:01.130 --> 01:28:04.540
I got an inkling of this
a couple of years ago when

01:28:04.540 --> 01:28:06.780
[? Alain ?] [? Renard, ?]
who some of you know,

01:28:06.780 --> 01:28:13.690
sent me an investment
brochure from Lehman Brothers,

01:28:13.690 --> 01:28:18.760
which was being sent out to
their prime investors or people

01:28:18.760 --> 01:28:20.500
who put a lot of
money in and so on.

01:28:20.500 --> 01:28:23.410
And it was about new
investment opportunities.

01:28:23.410 --> 01:28:25.430
And this one was about
what they called EMOs.

01:28:28.450 --> 01:28:31.040
I'll give you a second to
figure out what that means.

01:28:31.040 --> 01:28:34.240
But the point is, the prison
system's being privatized.

01:28:34.240 --> 01:28:36.240
The health system,
such as there was,

01:28:36.240 --> 01:28:39.460
is being given to insurance
companies with HMOs.

01:28:39.460 --> 01:28:41.620
There is still this residue--

01:28:41.620 --> 01:28:43.270
the educational system.

01:28:43.270 --> 01:28:45.580
So the next target
is going to be EMOs--

01:28:45.580 --> 01:28:48.130
educational management
organizations--

01:28:48.130 --> 01:28:51.240
which will be privately run--
publicly funded, of course.

01:28:51.240 --> 01:28:54.400
All this stuff gets publicly
funded in one way or another.

01:28:54.400 --> 01:28:56.760
But the profits are privatized.

01:28:56.760 --> 01:28:59.640
And that will
achieve great gains,

01:28:59.640 --> 01:29:05.150
for example, by hiring
teachers who are non-union,

01:29:05.150 --> 01:29:08.490
staff that's non-union, doesn't
have to be paid decent wages,

01:29:08.490 --> 01:29:12.490
doesn't require any security,
temps, and so on and so forth.

01:29:12.490 --> 01:29:16.410
So it's very efficient
by some measure.

01:29:16.410 --> 01:29:19.470
And also, it'll be a way of
breaking down this lingering

01:29:19.470 --> 01:29:21.840
sense of solidarity
and mutual support

01:29:21.840 --> 01:29:24.090
that the public school
system provides, just

01:29:24.090 --> 01:29:25.920
by its very existence.

01:29:25.920 --> 01:29:29.670
So I think you can expect
a lot of such attacks--

01:29:29.670 --> 01:29:34.530
not just on that, but on every
other residue of human life

01:29:34.530 --> 01:29:40.950
that reflects any value other
than individual maximization

01:29:40.950 --> 01:29:43.470
of invented wants.

01:29:43.470 --> 01:29:46.434
Anything beyond that is
fundamentally unacceptable.

01:29:50.100 --> 01:29:52.370
AUDIENCE: Just for
your information,

01:29:52.370 --> 01:29:56.500
this talk has apparently
filled three overflow rooms.

01:29:56.500 --> 01:29:57.510
So congratulations.

01:29:57.510 --> 01:29:59.659
[LAUGHTER]

01:29:59.659 --> 01:30:01.450
NOAM CHOMSKY: It's a
reflection of the fact

01:30:01.450 --> 01:30:03.180
that a lot of people are
worried about these issues

01:30:03.180 --> 01:30:04.138
and have a right to be.

01:30:04.138 --> 01:30:05.500
AUDIENCE: Exactly.

01:30:05.500 --> 01:30:06.000
I think so.

01:30:06.000 --> 01:30:07.110
NOAM CHOMSKY: The
only thing to do

01:30:07.110 --> 01:30:09.600
is to go beyond being worried
about them to doing something

01:30:09.600 --> 01:30:10.140
about them.

01:30:10.140 --> 01:30:11.417
Because it doesn't
have to happen.

01:30:11.417 --> 01:30:12.250
AUDIENCE: Very good.

01:30:12.250 --> 01:30:15.121
[APPLAUSE]

01:30:15.214 --> 01:30:17.130
And that's part of what
I'd like to ask about.

01:30:17.130 --> 01:30:17.440
NOAM CHOMSKY: Yeah.

01:30:17.440 --> 01:30:18.400
AUDIENCE: I'd like
to ask you to turn

01:30:18.400 --> 01:30:20.275
your attention, in the
light of your comments

01:30:20.275 --> 01:30:24.160
tonight, to Latin
America, and specifically

01:30:24.160 --> 01:30:27.850
to the country of
Colombia and what's

01:30:27.850 --> 01:30:30.370
going on there and the
purported rationale

01:30:30.370 --> 01:30:35.140
for the ratcheting up
of US military support--

01:30:35.140 --> 01:30:38.110
the purported rationale
being the supposed war

01:30:38.110 --> 01:30:39.700
against drugs--

01:30:39.700 --> 01:30:42.200
and a neighboring
country to Colombia,

01:30:42.200 --> 01:30:45.070
which is the nation of Peru.

01:30:45.070 --> 01:30:46.840
And I have a particular
reason for wanting

01:30:46.840 --> 01:30:49.750
to ask you about Peru,
which is that there

01:30:49.750 --> 01:30:51.670
is an American woman
who was a student here

01:30:51.670 --> 01:30:55.030
at MIT whose name is
Lori Berenson, who's

01:30:55.030 --> 01:30:57.490
been in prison in Peru
for over three years

01:30:57.490 --> 01:30:59.890
now without a trial.

01:30:59.890 --> 01:31:04.060
And perhaps in your comments
about Colombia and Peru,

01:31:04.060 --> 01:31:07.849
you might also have something
to say about her situation.

01:31:07.849 --> 01:31:09.890
NOAM CHOMSKY: Well, there's
really two questions.

01:31:09.890 --> 01:31:11.740
One is about Lori
Berenson, who was

01:31:11.740 --> 01:31:13.240
a student here a
couple of years ago

01:31:13.240 --> 01:31:18.440
and is now in a prison
in Peru without trial.

01:31:18.440 --> 01:31:22.720
And that's something we really
could do something about.

01:31:22.720 --> 01:31:24.650
I mean, pressure by
the US government--

01:31:24.650 --> 01:31:28.450
meaning pressure from the
population on the US government

01:31:28.450 --> 01:31:29.290
on Peru--

01:31:29.290 --> 01:31:32.200
would deal with that case.

01:31:32.200 --> 01:31:34.180
No question about that.

01:31:34.180 --> 01:31:36.270
So that's a simple choice.

01:31:36.270 --> 01:31:38.720
You can do things
about it or not.

01:31:38.720 --> 01:31:40.630
The choice is open.

01:31:40.630 --> 01:31:44.620
The more general question
about the whole Andean region--

01:31:44.620 --> 01:31:52.460
Colombia, Peru, Bolivia-- has
to do with several things.

01:31:52.460 --> 01:31:59.740
Colombia, which the point is
that the US is now increasing

01:31:59.740 --> 01:32:01.060
its military aid to Colombia.

01:32:01.060 --> 01:32:03.435
Actually, Colombia has been
one of the leading recipients

01:32:03.435 --> 01:32:06.340
of US military aid,
right through the '90s,

01:32:06.340 --> 01:32:08.030
going up under Clinton.

01:32:08.030 --> 01:32:12.040
It's also the most violent
country in the hemisphere.

01:32:12.040 --> 01:32:16.060
There are State
Department reports

01:32:16.060 --> 01:32:19.930
that would suggest that about
10 people a day are victims

01:32:19.930 --> 01:32:22.870
of political violence,
has well over a million

01:32:22.870 --> 01:32:24.910
internal refugees.

01:32:24.910 --> 01:32:28.120
Most of the violence is
attributed to paramilitaries,

01:32:28.120 --> 01:32:29.770
including by the
State Department.

01:32:29.770 --> 01:32:31.960
Paramilitaries are
essentially a front

01:32:31.960 --> 01:32:35.500
for the military
and the landowners.

01:32:35.500 --> 01:32:38.530
Other atrocities of
the military directly.

01:32:38.530 --> 01:32:41.080
There are also
guerrilla atrocities.

01:32:41.080 --> 01:32:44.860
But the large majority are
government and paramilitary.

01:32:44.860 --> 01:32:47.320
They're all connected
in complicated and not

01:32:47.320 --> 01:32:52.480
so complicated ways to land
ownership, resource control,

01:32:52.480 --> 01:32:56.470
now, in the last years,
narco trafficking and so on.

01:32:56.470 --> 01:32:59.800
And the US is fighting what it
calls a "drug war," including,

01:32:59.800 --> 01:33:01.730
incidentally,
biological warfare.

01:33:01.730 --> 01:33:04.367
So new fungicides
are being introduced.

01:33:04.367 --> 01:33:05.950
Nobody knows what
effect they'll have.

01:33:05.950 --> 01:33:07.720
They're supposed to kill coca.

01:33:07.720 --> 01:33:12.220
But if something else
happens, not our business.

01:33:12.220 --> 01:33:17.740
Crop spraying, just government
and paramilitary violence.

01:33:17.740 --> 01:33:20.410
Everyone knows that the
paramilitaries and the military

01:33:20.410 --> 01:33:22.810
are tied up with
the drug traffic.

01:33:22.810 --> 01:33:25.660
Just a couple of months ago,
a Colombian Air Force plane

01:33:25.660 --> 01:33:31.859
was picked up, one of the Air
Force commanders full of drugs.

01:33:31.859 --> 01:33:34.150
This is happening all through
the region, incidentally.

01:33:34.150 --> 01:33:40.780
The presidential jet of
the president of Nicaragua

01:33:40.780 --> 01:33:44.920
was recently the subject of
a major scandal in Nicaragua,

01:33:44.920 --> 01:33:48.116
kept out of the press
here because he's our boy.

01:33:48.116 --> 01:33:51.010
And it was found
that the jet was

01:33:51.010 --> 01:33:57.040
being used for drug transport
from Miami to wherever, going

01:33:57.040 --> 01:34:00.040
through Nicaragua, which has
become a major drug transit

01:34:00.040 --> 01:34:03.520
area ever since the US
took it over in 1990.

01:34:03.520 --> 01:34:05.920
But Colombia is a
much bigger one.

01:34:05.920 --> 01:34:09.670
The whole drug war-- you can't
really talk about the tactics,

01:34:09.670 --> 01:34:12.760
in my opinion, because the whole
thing is conceived in a way

01:34:12.760 --> 01:34:14.110
which--

01:34:14.110 --> 01:34:17.800
it has a kind of irrationality
from the point of view of those

01:34:17.800 --> 01:34:20.230
who are executing the war.

01:34:20.230 --> 01:34:24.560
But it has no rationality
from a human point of view.

01:34:24.560 --> 01:34:27.670
So from a human point of
view, the drug problem

01:34:27.670 --> 01:34:30.040
is in the United States.

01:34:30.040 --> 01:34:34.420
In fact, the US is maybe
the major producer or one

01:34:34.420 --> 01:34:38.590
of the major producers
of the drug--

01:34:38.590 --> 01:34:41.560
the substances, it's called--
that's most rapidly increasing,

01:34:41.560 --> 01:34:42.580
namely synthetics.

01:34:42.580 --> 01:34:44.170
They're made right here.

01:34:44.170 --> 01:34:46.630
Even the drugs that are
produced in the Andes

01:34:46.630 --> 01:34:50.620
are produced with US chemicals.

01:34:50.620 --> 01:34:53.920
The money mostly goes
through US banks.

01:34:53.920 --> 01:34:56.180
The "drug problem,"
as it's called,

01:34:56.180 --> 01:34:58.820
is largely a
manufactured problem.

01:34:58.820 --> 01:35:03.970
And so if you want to
figure out what it's like,

01:35:03.970 --> 01:35:05.320
take, say, marijuana.

01:35:05.320 --> 01:35:09.660
There has never been any medical
evidence presented that--

01:35:09.660 --> 01:35:11.530
it's probably not
good for you, just

01:35:11.530 --> 01:35:14.240
like meat isn't good for
you or coffee or something.

01:35:14.240 --> 01:35:14.740
[LAUGHTER]

01:35:14.740 --> 01:35:16.810
I'm sure it's not good for you.

01:35:16.810 --> 01:35:20.620
But the evidence about
it is extremely slim.

01:35:20.620 --> 01:35:22.630
Certainly no
medical evidence was

01:35:22.630 --> 01:35:25.645
presented to justify
its being criminalized.

01:35:25.645 --> 01:35:27.680
And in fact, the American
Medical Association

01:35:27.680 --> 01:35:29.230
never favored it.

01:35:29.230 --> 01:35:31.060
The criminalization
was a different thing.

01:35:31.060 --> 01:35:33.940
It has an interesting history.

01:35:33.940 --> 01:35:36.820
Forgetting the criminalization,
the peak use of marijuana,

01:35:36.820 --> 01:35:41.060
as I'm remembering figures-- so
they may not be exactly right,

01:35:41.060 --> 01:35:42.250
but something like this--

01:35:42.250 --> 01:35:44.290
was around 1980.

01:35:44.290 --> 01:35:47.600
And the criminalization
level was very low.

01:35:47.600 --> 01:35:49.520
The reason was quite
straightforward.

01:35:49.520 --> 01:35:52.360
It was people like
you, you know.

01:35:52.360 --> 01:35:56.080
And they don't get sent to jail,
or their parents make a fuss

01:35:56.080 --> 01:35:57.890
and do things
about it and so on.

01:35:57.890 --> 01:36:01.570
So those guys don't go to jail.

01:36:01.570 --> 01:36:05.320
Through the 1980s, two
things were happening.

01:36:05.320 --> 01:36:08.290
Use of substances over
a very broad range

01:36:08.290 --> 01:36:11.480
was declining among
educated people.

01:36:11.480 --> 01:36:12.820
And that's very broad--

01:36:12.820 --> 01:36:18.790
marijuana, hard drugs,
coffee, tobacco, red meat,

01:36:18.790 --> 01:36:19.890
big spectrum of stuff.

01:36:19.890 --> 01:36:20.390
[LAUGHTER]

01:36:20.390 --> 01:36:21.680
It was declining.

01:36:21.680 --> 01:36:24.800
I mean, all sorts of
changes in lifestyle.

01:36:24.800 --> 01:36:27.610
But it was remaining steady
or maybe even increasing

01:36:27.610 --> 01:36:28.510
among poor people.

01:36:31.090 --> 01:36:36.190
The United States is almost
alone in the industrial world

01:36:36.190 --> 01:36:38.290
in that, though it has
statistics on everything,

01:36:38.290 --> 01:36:40.870
it does not have
statistics on class.

01:36:40.870 --> 01:36:42.730
That's an ideological decision.

01:36:42.730 --> 01:36:45.850
So you can't get figures on a
class out of the Census Bureau.

01:36:45.850 --> 01:36:48.580
You have to reconstruct
them in some fashion,

01:36:48.580 --> 01:36:49.700
unlike other countries.

01:36:49.700 --> 01:36:52.740
But there is a close
class/race correlation.

01:36:52.740 --> 01:36:56.950
And among the poor and hence,
heavily among minorities,

01:36:56.950 --> 01:36:59.500
there was an either
steady or increasing use

01:36:59.500 --> 01:37:00.400
of the substances.

01:37:00.400 --> 01:37:02.650
Well, you take a look
at the trend lines

01:37:02.650 --> 01:37:05.200
and ask yourself when
the drug war was called.

01:37:05.200 --> 01:37:07.570
And it was approximately
when they crossed.

01:37:07.570 --> 01:37:09.730
So you could predict
victory in the drug

01:37:09.730 --> 01:37:12.621
war among educated
people because the trend

01:37:12.621 --> 01:37:13.870
lines were already going down.

01:37:13.870 --> 01:37:15.970
So they'll continue going down.

01:37:15.970 --> 01:37:18.160
And you could also predict
what was pointed out

01:37:18.160 --> 01:37:21.730
by Senator Moynihan-- not
my favorite character,

01:37:21.730 --> 01:37:23.950
but he does happen to be
one of the few senators who

01:37:23.950 --> 01:37:25.910
paid attention to
social statistics

01:37:25.910 --> 01:37:27.370
and a social scientist.

01:37:27.370 --> 01:37:29.620
And he pointed out
that we are determining

01:37:29.620 --> 01:37:36.640
to declare a rise in
crime or something

01:37:36.640 --> 01:37:38.690
like that among minorities.

01:37:38.690 --> 01:37:42.670
We're determining to declare
a crime wave among minorities.

01:37:42.670 --> 01:37:45.760
That's exactly what we're doing
by declaring the drug war.

01:37:45.760 --> 01:37:47.110
And it was done.

01:37:47.110 --> 01:37:49.810
And I think it was done for
reasons of social policy.

01:37:49.810 --> 01:37:52.450
This is a superfluous
population.

01:37:52.450 --> 01:37:59.680
They are the counterpart of the
Colombian disposable people.

01:37:59.680 --> 01:38:03.727
And you want to crime war
among them for two reasons.

01:38:03.727 --> 01:38:05.560
For one thing, because
you get rid of them--

01:38:05.560 --> 01:38:06.910
toss them into jail.

01:38:06.910 --> 01:38:09.285
And for another thing, because
you can frighten everybody

01:38:09.285 --> 01:38:12.970
else, and that's important.

01:38:12.970 --> 01:38:16.010
Crime in general, in
the United States,

01:38:16.010 --> 01:38:19.670
it's kind of a
political construct.

01:38:19.670 --> 01:38:21.290
Crime in the United
States is not

01:38:21.290 --> 01:38:24.020
very different from other
industrial countries,

01:38:24.020 --> 01:38:25.670
with the single
exception of killings

01:38:25.670 --> 01:38:28.520
with guns, which is a
special thing about the gun

01:38:28.520 --> 01:38:29.869
laws and the gun culture.

01:38:29.869 --> 01:38:31.910
But apart from that, it's
kind of at the high end

01:38:31.910 --> 01:38:34.220
of other industrial societies.

01:38:34.220 --> 01:38:35.940
Fear of crime, on
the other hand,

01:38:35.940 --> 01:38:38.300
is way beyond other societies.

01:38:38.300 --> 01:38:39.870
And it's manufactured.

01:38:39.870 --> 01:38:42.620
It's manufactured
by politicians.

01:38:42.620 --> 01:38:45.590
It's one of the very few
countries in which politicians

01:38:45.590 --> 01:38:49.940
stand up and debate about who's
going to be tougher on crime.

01:38:49.940 --> 01:38:53.490
Elsewhere, crime is
considered a problem.

01:38:53.490 --> 01:38:55.260
It's like cancer or something.

01:38:55.260 --> 01:38:56.640
You try to deal with it.

01:38:56.640 --> 01:38:59.120
But you don't decide who's
going to be tougher on it.

01:38:59.120 --> 01:39:01.100
In the United States,
you argue about who's

01:39:01.100 --> 01:39:02.630
going to be tougher on it.

01:39:02.630 --> 01:39:05.330
Back around 1980,
the United States

01:39:05.330 --> 01:39:07.670
was pretty much like other
industrial societies,

01:39:07.670 --> 01:39:11.300
again, sort of at high end
in incarceration rates.

01:39:11.300 --> 01:39:13.610
Since then, it's gone way up.

01:39:13.610 --> 01:39:16.100
By the end of the Reagan years,
it was like 5 to 10 times

01:39:16.100 --> 01:39:19.130
as high, still going
up, mostly a reflection

01:39:19.130 --> 01:39:23.030
of the war on drugs, of
mostly victimless crimes--

01:39:23.030 --> 01:39:25.110
possession of some
sort or another--

01:39:25.110 --> 01:39:27.260
which is given
extraordinary sentences.

01:39:27.260 --> 01:39:29.540
That's one of the
human rights violations

01:39:29.540 --> 01:39:32.180
for which the United
States is regularly

01:39:32.180 --> 01:39:34.700
condemned by the human
rights organizations--

01:39:34.700 --> 01:39:39.300
the outrageous sentencing
for victimless crimes,

01:39:39.300 --> 01:39:43.400
which is just targeting poor
and the disposable people,

01:39:43.400 --> 01:39:44.930
and frightening the rest.

01:39:44.930 --> 01:39:47.120
So, instrument of
social control.

01:39:47.120 --> 01:39:49.820
You go over to the
Andes, the places

01:39:49.820 --> 01:39:51.350
where coke is being
produced-- well,

01:39:51.350 --> 01:39:53.250
there you find another story.

01:39:53.250 --> 01:39:56.150
So Colombia, for example,
was once a wheat exporter.

01:39:56.150 --> 01:39:58.910
Now, why isn't it a
wheat exporter anymore?

01:39:58.910 --> 01:40:04.760
Well, the US flooded it with
subsidized US agro exports.

01:40:04.760 --> 01:40:07.820
It was called Food for
Peace back in the '50s.

01:40:07.820 --> 01:40:09.935
And it undermined the
Colombian wheat industry.

01:40:09.935 --> 01:40:12.110
They're not exporting anymore.

01:40:12.110 --> 01:40:14.090
Same thing happened elsewhere.

01:40:14.090 --> 01:40:16.700
Colombia is a coffee exporter.

01:40:16.700 --> 01:40:19.760
But coffee is not very
good for small peasants,

01:40:19.760 --> 01:40:21.500
for a very simple reason.

01:40:21.500 --> 01:40:25.430
The US refused to
permit arrangements

01:40:25.430 --> 01:40:28.070
among the producers
to keep prices stable.

01:40:28.070 --> 01:40:30.620
Now if prices fluctuate
all over the place,

01:40:30.620 --> 01:40:31.910
it doesn't hurt agribusiness.

01:40:31.910 --> 01:40:36.470
I mean, they own enough
other things and so on,

01:40:36.470 --> 01:40:39.780
so they can make out if
coffee prices go down.

01:40:39.780 --> 01:40:42.170
If you're a small peasant,
you can't do that.

01:40:42.170 --> 01:40:44.120
You've got to feed
your kids tomorrow.

01:40:44.120 --> 01:40:46.370
Price goes down too
low, you're dead.

01:40:46.370 --> 01:40:48.710
So that drove people out
of coffee production,

01:40:48.710 --> 01:40:50.810
out of wheat production,
out of other production.

01:40:50.810 --> 01:40:52.790
Well, where'd they go?

01:40:52.790 --> 01:40:55.280
Well, you know, they were
being taught a lesson.

01:40:55.280 --> 01:40:57.050
It's called
structural adjustment.

01:40:57.050 --> 01:41:00.590
All throughout neoliberalism,
all throughout this region,

01:41:00.590 --> 01:41:05.870
the lesson is, stop producing
things for local consumption,

01:41:05.870 --> 01:41:07.730
like, say, food.

01:41:07.730 --> 01:41:11.780
We can do that cheaper with
subsidized agro-business.

01:41:11.780 --> 01:41:13.750
You be a rational peasant.

01:41:13.750 --> 01:41:15.860
Produce for agro export.

01:41:15.860 --> 01:41:19.550
And produce what's going to make
the most money in agro export.

01:41:19.550 --> 01:41:23.790
That's a rational peasant
that's following the rules.

01:41:23.790 --> 01:41:26.177
Well, they did.

01:41:26.177 --> 01:41:27.260
You're a rational peasant.

01:41:27.260 --> 01:41:29.220
You produce coca.

01:41:29.220 --> 01:41:32.190
Grows well, makes a ton
of money, and so on.

01:41:32.190 --> 01:41:34.400
So one part of US
policy is trying

01:41:34.400 --> 01:41:37.750
to drive the population
towards producing drugs.

01:41:37.750 --> 01:41:39.500
And the other part
of US policy is

01:41:39.500 --> 01:41:41.510
to murder them if they do it.

01:41:41.510 --> 01:41:43.370
That's the support
for the military

01:41:43.370 --> 01:41:45.060
and the counterinsurgency
and so on.

01:41:45.060 --> 01:41:47.102
And those two policies
are going on side by side.

01:41:47.102 --> 01:41:49.560
And they don't have anything
to do with drugs in the United

01:41:49.560 --> 01:41:50.091
States.

01:41:50.091 --> 01:41:51.590
I mean, do that
stuff as much as you

01:41:51.590 --> 01:41:54.950
want, the drug price remains
stable, the quantity of drugs

01:41:54.950 --> 01:41:56.570
remains stable, and so on.

01:41:56.570 --> 01:41:59.120
This is an internal
US problem having

01:41:59.120 --> 01:42:03.130
to do with serious social
problems inside US society.

01:42:03.130 --> 01:42:05.360
And furthermore, it is
well-known and agreed

01:42:05.360 --> 01:42:09.260
by criminologists and other
specialists across the board

01:42:09.260 --> 01:42:12.110
that the best way to deal
with serious drug problems

01:42:12.110 --> 01:42:14.130
is education.

01:42:14.130 --> 01:42:16.040
But you don't spend
money on that,

01:42:16.040 --> 01:42:19.250
because that has no
use for social control

01:42:19.250 --> 01:42:21.560
or for eliminating
disposable people.

01:42:21.560 --> 01:42:24.680
So I don't think talking about
the tactics of the drug war

01:42:24.680 --> 01:42:26.490
makes a lot of sense.

01:42:26.490 --> 01:42:29.180
The whole thing is
constructed in a way which

01:42:29.180 --> 01:42:32.360
is totally insane,
except for rich people,

01:42:32.360 --> 01:42:35.540
like New York banks, where most
of the money flows through.

01:42:35.540 --> 01:42:37.770
Incidentally, what's
happening to the money?

01:42:37.770 --> 01:42:39.760
It's an interesting question.

01:42:39.760 --> 01:42:40.760
Of course, it's illegal.

01:42:40.760 --> 01:42:42.140
So nobody tracks it.

01:42:42.140 --> 01:42:46.760
Actually, you could track
it, especially with computers

01:42:46.760 --> 01:42:47.680
and so on.

01:42:47.680 --> 01:42:51.260
The Federal Reserve System,
which is well-regulated,

01:42:51.260 --> 01:42:54.740
requires notification
of large deposits.

01:42:54.740 --> 01:42:56.870
Last time I looked,
it was over $10,000.

01:42:56.870 --> 01:42:59.540
So if a lot of money
is suddenly coming in,

01:42:59.540 --> 01:43:01.110
they could tell right away.

01:43:01.110 --> 01:43:04.610
And in fact, there was an
effort by federal prosecutors

01:43:04.610 --> 01:43:11.390
back in the early '80s to carry
out some entrapment exercise

01:43:11.390 --> 01:43:13.024
against banks in
southern Florida,

01:43:13.024 --> 01:43:15.190
which were suddenly piling
up a huge amount of money

01:43:15.190 --> 01:43:17.710
for reasons that nobody doubted.

01:43:17.710 --> 01:43:18.900
It was called off, however.

01:43:18.900 --> 01:43:20.800
It was called
Operation Greenback.

01:43:20.800 --> 01:43:24.310
It was called off by the
Reagan administration's drug

01:43:24.310 --> 01:43:26.230
czar, George Bush.

01:43:26.230 --> 01:43:26.920
[LAUGHTER]

01:43:26.920 --> 01:43:28.330
That was called off.

01:43:28.330 --> 01:43:31.300
So the money's supposed
to come into US banks.

01:43:31.300 --> 01:43:32.660
Where does it go?

01:43:32.660 --> 01:43:36.190
Well, take a look sometime--

01:43:36.190 --> 01:43:37.000
this is a guess.

01:43:37.000 --> 01:43:38.290
Nobody's ever studied it.

01:43:38.290 --> 01:43:40.360
But the US Department
of Commerce

01:43:40.360 --> 01:43:42.070
does publish regularly--

01:43:42.070 --> 01:43:45.970
I think quarterly-- tons
of details about everything

01:43:45.970 --> 01:43:47.140
you can imagine.

01:43:47.140 --> 01:43:49.630
And one of them is foreign
direct investment--

01:43:49.630 --> 01:43:50.890
FDI.

01:43:50.890 --> 01:43:52.307
Take a look at the record.

01:43:52.307 --> 01:43:53.890
I haven't done this
for a year or two,

01:43:53.890 --> 01:43:56.400
but I was doing it for a
couple of years in mid '90s.

01:43:56.400 --> 01:43:59.620
You take a look at the
FDI for Latin America,

01:43:59.620 --> 01:44:01.440
for the hemisphere.

01:44:01.440 --> 01:44:03.070
Canada's a separate category.

01:44:03.070 --> 01:44:04.240
It's like Europe.

01:44:04.240 --> 01:44:06.970
But take the rest of the
hemisphere minus Canada,

01:44:06.970 --> 01:44:09.530
look at foreign
direct investment.

01:44:09.530 --> 01:44:12.310
It turns out that
regularly, about 25% of it

01:44:12.310 --> 01:44:14.470
was going to Bermuda.

01:44:14.470 --> 01:44:19.150
About 15% was going to
the British Islands.

01:44:19.150 --> 01:44:21.380
About 10% to Panama.

01:44:21.380 --> 01:44:23.620
So that's roughly
50% of what's called

01:44:23.620 --> 01:44:24.930
foreign direct investment.

01:44:24.930 --> 01:44:27.850
And this is the period
of the emerging markets.

01:44:27.850 --> 01:44:30.470
Everybody's excited about
the emerging markets.

01:44:30.470 --> 01:44:33.220
So about half of it was
going to these places.

01:44:33.220 --> 01:44:35.290
Well, what was it for?

01:44:35.290 --> 01:44:37.630
Certainly, it wasn't building
steel mills and automobile

01:44:37.630 --> 01:44:38.130
plants.

01:44:38.130 --> 01:44:40.780
There aren't many
of them in Bermuda.

01:44:40.780 --> 01:44:43.810
There is a kind of
benign interpretation.

01:44:43.810 --> 01:44:49.840
It's just a way of ripping off
the public by evading taxes.

01:44:49.840 --> 01:44:51.730
So that just harms poor people.

01:44:51.730 --> 01:44:54.010
There's a less benign
interpretation,

01:44:54.010 --> 01:44:57.090
which is possible and might be
discovered if anybody bothered

01:44:57.090 --> 01:44:58.050
to look at it.

01:44:58.050 --> 01:45:00.700
But I haven't been able
find one technical paper

01:45:00.700 --> 01:45:03.820
in the whole literature that
even looks at the topic.

01:45:03.820 --> 01:45:06.194
Some of you may know, if
there are some economists

01:45:06.194 --> 01:45:06.860
in the audience.

01:45:06.860 --> 01:45:08.300
But I can't find one.

01:45:08.300 --> 01:45:10.110
And no friends can find one.

01:45:10.110 --> 01:45:11.930
And it's not a minor phenomenon.

01:45:11.930 --> 01:45:16.150
It's 50% of US FDI and
the big emerging markets--

01:45:16.150 --> 01:45:17.430
big numbers.

01:45:17.430 --> 01:45:19.930
Well, make your own guesses.

01:45:19.930 --> 01:45:22.450
Anyhow, the whole
system is so corrupt

01:45:22.450 --> 01:45:26.650
and rotten from the bottom, that
to talk about tactical changes

01:45:26.650 --> 01:45:28.490
is just beside the point.

01:45:28.490 --> 01:45:31.330
The current policies,
exactly as you say,

01:45:31.330 --> 01:45:38.350
are to increase the military
and biological warfare

01:45:38.350 --> 01:45:41.350
component of these
policies abroad

01:45:41.350 --> 01:45:44.980
and to continue
the criminalization

01:45:44.980 --> 01:45:46.880
of the disposable
people it home.

01:45:46.880 --> 01:45:50.280
Those are the current policies.

01:45:50.280 --> 01:45:52.920
Lori Berenson is a very
unfortunate victim of this.

01:45:52.920 --> 01:45:54.720
I should say, the
violence in Colombia--

01:45:54.720 --> 01:45:57.730
you want to take a look at
it-- it goes back very deep.

01:45:57.730 --> 01:45:59.820
It goes back to a
socioeconomic system

01:45:59.820 --> 01:46:02.010
in which a tiny sector
of the population

01:46:02.010 --> 01:46:05.535
has most of the wealth in
a pretty wealthy country,

01:46:05.535 --> 01:46:08.850
and has extraordinarily high
poverty rates, starvation,

01:46:08.850 --> 01:46:09.782
and so on.

01:46:09.782 --> 01:46:11.490
That's where the
violence is coming from.

01:46:14.382 --> 01:46:15.828
Yeah.

01:46:15.828 --> 01:46:18.550
AUDIENCE: Professor,
you've taken us back

01:46:18.550 --> 01:46:22.630
to Indonesia's history.

01:46:22.630 --> 01:46:24.934
I find that very interesting.

01:46:24.934 --> 01:46:28.210
Issues in Indonesia
[? are ?] [? the ?] general

01:46:28.210 --> 01:46:31.300
[? election, ?] the
future of East Timor

01:46:31.300 --> 01:46:34.430
and the disintegration,
as you mentioned earlier.

01:46:34.430 --> 01:46:36.900
Can you elaborate on that, sir?

01:46:36.900 --> 01:46:42.575
And also, a couple of weeks ago,
the IMF apologized to Indonesia

01:46:42.575 --> 01:46:44.450
[? on ?] [? their ?]
[? mistakes ?] [? and ?]

01:46:44.450 --> 01:46:46.700
[? the ?] approach of getting
Indonesia of the current

01:46:46.700 --> 01:46:47.362
crisis.

01:46:47.362 --> 01:46:48.820
Can you also comment
[? on that? ?]

01:46:48.820 --> 01:46:49.785
NOAM CHOMSKY: I think
it was the World Bank.

01:46:49.785 --> 01:46:50.790
AUDIENCE: The World Bank, yeah.

01:46:50.790 --> 01:46:51.640
NOAM CHOMSKY: Yeah.

01:46:51.640 --> 01:46:54.490
The World Bank
published a report

01:46:54.490 --> 01:46:56.740
saying they had made
all sorts of mistakes.

01:46:56.740 --> 01:47:01.234
They were overenthusiastic
in their commitment

01:47:01.234 --> 01:47:02.650
to all these
wonderful things that

01:47:02.650 --> 01:47:03.920
were happening in Indonesia.

01:47:03.920 --> 01:47:06.920
And being a little
overenthusiastic,

01:47:06.920 --> 01:47:09.290
they kind of played down
the corruption and so on.

01:47:09.290 --> 01:47:12.820
And that's not quite the story,
but yeah, kind of interesting.

01:47:12.820 --> 01:47:15.330
The fact is that the
World Bank and the IMF

01:47:15.330 --> 01:47:19.630
were praising
Indonesia to the skies,

01:47:19.630 --> 01:47:23.200
along with Thailand,
Malaysia, South

01:47:23.200 --> 01:47:25.900
Korea, for their solid
economic fundamentals

01:47:25.900 --> 01:47:28.810
and the way they'd been
following all the rules and all

01:47:28.810 --> 01:47:30.820
the magnificent things
they've been doing.

01:47:30.820 --> 01:47:34.750
And to their dismay,
the major publications

01:47:34.750 --> 01:47:39.030
appeared exactly as the
whole system was crashing.

01:47:39.030 --> 01:47:41.320
It's not the first time
that's happened, incidentally.

01:47:41.320 --> 01:47:43.090
But it was a pretty
dramatic case.

01:47:43.090 --> 01:47:45.850
So they've been kind of
running backwards ever since.

01:47:45.850 --> 01:47:48.820
And yes, they did say they've
made a lot of mistakes.

01:47:48.820 --> 01:47:50.920
That's mild.

01:47:50.920 --> 01:47:54.460
They knew what was going on
and decided they liked it.

01:47:54.460 --> 01:47:57.400
Because rich people in Indonesia
were profiting and rich people

01:47:57.400 --> 01:47:58.330
abroad were profiting.

01:47:58.330 --> 01:47:59.680
So what's the fuss?

01:47:59.680 --> 01:48:01.120
No crisis.

01:48:01.120 --> 01:48:02.290
Now there's a crisis.

01:48:02.290 --> 01:48:04.180
So they're, yeah,
looking back, and so on.

01:48:04.180 --> 01:48:06.304
And they want to sort of
reconstruct the situation.

01:48:06.304 --> 01:48:09.640
And they don't know how,
because it may be that Indonesia

01:48:09.640 --> 01:48:11.910
will simply come apart.

01:48:11.910 --> 01:48:15.640
There's two issues
that you raised.

01:48:15.640 --> 01:48:18.520
One is the possible
disintegration of Indonesia.

01:48:18.520 --> 01:48:21.360
The other is the specific
case of East Timor.

01:48:21.360 --> 01:48:26.890
The East Timor case is
difficult to figure out.

01:48:26.890 --> 01:48:29.470
I mean, Indonesia--
let's take that one.

01:48:29.470 --> 01:48:32.380
Indonesia has
agreed to grant what

01:48:32.380 --> 01:48:38.200
they call "autonomy" within
the Indonesian federation

01:48:38.200 --> 01:48:39.640
or something.

01:48:39.640 --> 01:48:42.580
Beyond that, there's
a policy split,

01:48:42.580 --> 01:48:46.570
which may reflect two different
tracks of Indonesian policy,

01:48:46.570 --> 01:48:50.140
or it may be two sides of
the same cynical policy.

01:48:50.140 --> 01:48:51.250
Nobody knows.

01:48:51.250 --> 01:48:54.310
In fact, I suspect the people
making the policy probably

01:48:54.310 --> 01:48:54.910
don't know.

01:48:54.910 --> 01:48:57.250
They're waiting to see
how things turn out.

01:48:57.250 --> 01:49:00.820
One policy track would
lead towards independence.

01:49:00.820 --> 01:49:05.260
The other policy track leads
towards increasing violence.

01:49:05.260 --> 01:49:08.020
So while Indonesia
is talking about,

01:49:08.020 --> 01:49:10.300
OK, you guys can be
independent if you want,

01:49:10.300 --> 01:49:14.770
it's also at the same time
organizing paramilitaries,

01:49:14.770 --> 01:49:16.120
arming them.

01:49:16.120 --> 01:49:17.990
They're carrying out violence.

01:49:17.990 --> 01:49:20.590
In fact, they're killing
people, driving people out

01:49:20.590 --> 01:49:21.490
by the thousands.

01:49:21.490 --> 01:49:24.940
And the place is exploding.

01:49:24.940 --> 01:49:26.920
The same-- going back
to the question--

01:49:26.920 --> 01:49:28.630
is happening in Colombia.

01:49:28.630 --> 01:49:30.820
It's not an unusual pattern--

01:49:30.820 --> 01:49:33.130
a move towards
peace negotiations

01:49:33.130 --> 01:49:35.620
and at the same time
unleashing the paramilitaries

01:49:35.620 --> 01:49:37.630
with the military
right in the background

01:49:37.630 --> 01:49:39.790
to tear the place apart.

01:49:39.790 --> 01:49:42.550
If there's going to be
negotiations of some kind,

01:49:42.550 --> 01:49:47.110
you want to make sure that your
thugs have it under control.

01:49:47.110 --> 01:49:50.500
Those two paths quite
commonly go side by side.

01:49:53.080 --> 01:49:56.110
You find it elsewhere, too.

01:49:56.110 --> 01:49:59.200
In this case, I think
it's hard to say.

01:49:59.200 --> 01:50:01.760
I mean, it could be two
sides of the same policy.

01:50:01.760 --> 01:50:04.087
It could be
conflicting policies.

01:50:04.087 --> 01:50:05.920
It could be just
uncertainty that they don't

01:50:05.920 --> 01:50:07.837
know what's going to happen.

01:50:07.837 --> 01:50:09.670
I don't think there's
much point speculating

01:50:09.670 --> 01:50:11.510
about this, frankly.

01:50:11.510 --> 01:50:14.740
As I said, I doubt that the
Indonesian generals know.

01:50:14.740 --> 01:50:16.750
They're waiting to
see what happens.

01:50:16.750 --> 01:50:19.390
What is significant
here, as always,

01:50:19.390 --> 01:50:21.780
is that what we do about
it can make a difference--

01:50:21.780 --> 01:50:23.360
a big difference.

01:50:23.360 --> 01:50:26.410
So one of the factors and
undoubtedly a major, if not

01:50:26.410 --> 01:50:28.270
decisive, factor will
be how the United

01:50:28.270 --> 01:50:30.604
States responds to all of this.

01:50:30.604 --> 01:50:32.770
And that's the one factor
we can do something about.

01:50:32.770 --> 01:50:34.870
So there's no point
speculating about the others.

01:50:34.870 --> 01:50:37.210
We should ask, what
we do about these?

01:50:37.210 --> 01:50:41.310
As for Indonesia breaking
up, it could happen.

01:50:41.310 --> 01:50:44.200
There's a strong independence
movement in Aceh.

01:50:44.200 --> 01:50:48.730
The, what they call
Irian Jaya, West Papua,

01:50:48.730 --> 01:50:52.480
was given to Indonesia over the
objections of its population.

01:50:52.480 --> 01:50:53.980
That has an
independence movement.

01:50:53.980 --> 01:50:57.670
There's an independence
movement in the Malaccas.

01:50:57.670 --> 01:50:59.230
It could go all sorts of ways.

01:50:59.230 --> 01:51:03.040
Don't forget, that whole
thing, like most of the world,

01:51:03.040 --> 01:51:06.710
that was just patched together
by European imperialism.

01:51:06.710 --> 01:51:08.570
They put it the other
for their reasons,

01:51:08.570 --> 01:51:12.080
not for the reasons
of the countries.

01:51:12.080 --> 01:51:15.040
So if you sort of randomly
broke up the United States

01:51:15.040 --> 01:51:17.980
and put them under different
flags and so on and so forth,

01:51:17.980 --> 01:51:21.290
yeah, you'd have plenty
of violence after a while.

01:51:21.290 --> 01:51:24.280
Because these breakdowns don't
have anything to do with what's

01:51:24.280 --> 01:51:26.470
happening on the ground.

01:51:26.470 --> 01:51:28.780
Like for the Middle
East, probably the only

01:51:28.780 --> 01:51:30.910
semi-rational structure
that's been around

01:51:30.910 --> 01:51:32.410
in the last couple
of thousand years

01:51:32.410 --> 01:51:37.450
was the Ottoman Empire,
which allowed local autonomy,

01:51:37.450 --> 01:51:42.310
to a large extent, within
a rather loose and corrupt

01:51:42.310 --> 01:51:44.230
imperial framework.

01:51:44.230 --> 01:51:45.220
OK.

01:51:45.220 --> 01:51:47.370
People were freer under
the Ottoman Empire

01:51:47.370 --> 01:51:48.370
than they've been since.

01:51:48.370 --> 01:51:51.880
They could go from one place
to another, for example.

01:51:51.880 --> 01:51:55.720
But they had national treatment,
like General Electric wants.

01:51:59.310 --> 01:52:02.960
And that's probably right
for most of the world.

01:52:02.960 --> 01:52:05.460
Actually, it's probably
right for Europe, too.

01:52:05.460 --> 01:52:07.990
I mean, the borders
in Europe are simply

01:52:07.990 --> 01:52:12.910
the result of hundreds of
years of mutual massacre

01:52:12.910 --> 01:52:15.310
and the most violent
and barbaric corner

01:52:15.310 --> 01:52:19.270
of the world, which is
exactly what Europe was.

01:52:19.270 --> 01:52:22.870
For hundreds of years, the
highest goal of Europeans

01:52:22.870 --> 01:52:25.240
was to slaughter one another.

01:52:25.240 --> 01:52:28.900
And the only reason that
came to an end in 1945

01:52:28.900 --> 01:52:31.240
is because they all
understood that they

01:52:31.240 --> 01:52:34.120
had reached a level of violence
such that the next time they

01:52:34.120 --> 01:52:36.670
tried, that's the
end for everybody.

01:52:36.670 --> 01:52:39.160
That's why you have political
scientists writing books

01:52:39.160 --> 01:52:42.900
on how there aren't wars among
democratic societies and so on.

01:52:42.900 --> 01:52:45.340
Yeah, they're smart
enough to know

01:52:45.340 --> 01:52:47.200
that they try once
more what they've

01:52:47.200 --> 01:52:50.140
been doing for the last 500
years, and everybody's dead.

01:52:50.140 --> 01:52:52.720
So right now they're
not fighting wars.

01:52:52.720 --> 01:52:55.120
But the borders that
are left from these wars

01:52:55.120 --> 01:52:57.490
are pretty irrational.

01:52:57.490 --> 01:52:59.830
And in fact, along with
European unification,

01:52:59.830 --> 01:53:03.040
you're also finding
devolution, so pressures

01:53:03.040 --> 01:53:07.320
towards regional autonomy, which
probably makes a lot of sense

01:53:07.320 --> 01:53:09.565
because the borders
didn't make any sense.

01:53:09.565 --> 01:53:11.440
And see, it's different
in the United States.

01:53:11.440 --> 01:53:12.481
I mean, here it was easy.

01:53:12.481 --> 01:53:14.380
You just wipe out the
indigenous population

01:53:14.380 --> 01:53:17.020
and then settle it with a
fairly homogeneous population.

01:53:17.020 --> 01:53:19.200
OK, don't have big problems.

01:53:19.200 --> 01:53:22.240
But Europe didn't
develop that way,

01:53:22.240 --> 01:53:23.800
and nor did the
rest of the world.

01:53:23.800 --> 01:53:28.000
It was all imposed on them
by competing imperialisms.

01:53:28.000 --> 01:53:29.740
The Dutch East Indies
are what the Dutch

01:53:29.740 --> 01:53:32.440
were able to hold onto.

01:53:32.440 --> 01:53:37.180
And what form it takes,
I don't think anybody can

01:53:37.180 --> 01:53:40.562
[? prescribe. ?] That's for
them to work out somehow,

01:53:40.562 --> 01:53:41.506
and not easy.

01:53:46.226 --> 01:53:49.120
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] free
trade has widened the gap

01:53:49.120 --> 01:53:50.174
between North and South?

01:53:50.174 --> 01:53:51.590
NOAM CHOMSKY: I
haven't said that.

01:53:51.590 --> 01:53:53.850
I don't think there
is much free trade.

01:53:53.850 --> 01:53:58.670
In fact, a standard figure
among international economists

01:53:58.670 --> 01:54:01.460
is that maybe 15%
of world trade could

01:54:01.460 --> 01:54:02.930
be called "free" in some sense.

01:54:02.930 --> 01:54:06.180
AUDIENCE: But net
gains from free trade

01:54:06.180 --> 01:54:07.400
are being appropriated by--

01:54:07.400 --> 01:54:08.600
NOAM CHOMSKY: Trade,
not free trade.

01:54:08.600 --> 01:54:09.225
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:54:09.225 --> 01:54:11.195
From trade are being
appropriated by the North.

01:54:11.195 --> 01:54:13.444
[? Do you ?] [? think that ?]
could be counterbalanced

01:54:13.444 --> 01:54:14.872
by [? freer ?]
movement of labor?

01:54:14.872 --> 01:54:15.830
NOAM CHOMSKY: Oh, yeah.

01:54:15.830 --> 01:54:17.370
In fact, if anybody believed--

01:54:17.370 --> 01:54:19.670
I mean everybody is supposed
to admire Adam Smith.

01:54:19.670 --> 01:54:21.170
But you're not
supposed to read him.

01:54:21.170 --> 01:54:22.250
That's very crucial.

01:54:22.250 --> 01:54:22.940
[LAUGHTER]

01:54:22.940 --> 01:54:28.010
If you read Adam Smith, one
bad thing that you discover

01:54:28.010 --> 01:54:30.350
is that his argument
for the market

01:54:30.350 --> 01:54:35.210
was based on the assumption
that under conditions of liberty

01:54:35.210 --> 01:54:38.750
a market will lead to
equality, which is obviously

01:54:38.750 --> 01:54:39.830
a desideratum.

01:54:39.830 --> 01:54:42.080
So therefore, he argued,
markets are good.

01:54:42.080 --> 01:54:44.090
If it doesn't lead
to equality, out.

01:54:44.090 --> 01:54:46.340
You'll also found out he was
against division of labor

01:54:46.340 --> 01:54:48.500
and all sorts of other things
you're not supposed to know.

01:54:48.500 --> 01:54:50.208
But one thing you're
not supposed to know

01:54:50.208 --> 01:54:54.470
is that free trade is
based on what he called

01:54:54.470 --> 01:54:56.754
"free circulation of labor."

01:54:56.754 --> 01:54:57.630
OK.

01:54:57.630 --> 01:55:00.657
Well, he wasn't thinking so
much about crossing borders

01:55:00.657 --> 01:55:02.240
in those days, because
nobody was even

01:55:02.240 --> 01:55:05.330
thinking of capital crossing
border much in those days.

01:55:05.330 --> 01:55:06.770
But the principle is there.

01:55:06.770 --> 01:55:09.380
So surely you don't have
anything like free trade

01:55:09.380 --> 01:55:11.900
unless you have free circulation
of labor around the world.

01:55:11.900 --> 01:55:14.780
Otherwise, you just
don't have labor markets.

01:55:14.780 --> 01:55:16.400
I mean, we're not
supposed to think

01:55:16.400 --> 01:55:18.560
about that, along with
a lot of other things.

01:55:18.560 --> 01:55:20.150
But yeah, that's true.

01:55:20.150 --> 01:55:22.200
Should you have free
circulation of labor?

01:55:22.200 --> 01:55:24.770
Well, that raises all
kind of questions--

01:55:24.770 --> 01:55:26.570
questions about how
people want to preserve

01:55:26.570 --> 01:55:28.400
their own communities,
let's say.

01:55:28.400 --> 01:55:30.320
But then of course,
those very same questions

01:55:30.320 --> 01:55:32.065
show up with
movement of capital.

01:55:32.065 --> 01:55:33.440
It's just that
you're not allowed

01:55:33.440 --> 01:55:35.880
to raise them in the case
of movement of capital.

01:55:35.880 --> 01:55:37.350
OK.

01:55:37.350 --> 01:55:38.610
So they're hard questions.

01:55:38.610 --> 01:55:41.090
I don't think they
have trivial answers.

01:55:41.090 --> 01:55:43.654
But you can't seriously
talk about free trade,

01:55:43.654 --> 01:55:45.320
unless you have free
movement of people.

01:55:45.320 --> 01:55:48.890
That's one of the many reasons
why the talk about free trade

01:55:48.890 --> 01:55:49.830
is mostly a fraud.

01:55:52.480 --> 01:55:53.740
It's an ideological weapon.

01:55:53.740 --> 01:55:56.870
It's not a descriptive fact.

01:55:56.870 --> 01:56:00.300
And there are many
reasons, not just that one.

01:56:00.300 --> 01:56:00.800
Yeah.

01:56:00.800 --> 01:56:04.130
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I just
wondered what's, in your view,

01:56:04.130 --> 01:56:07.355
the reason for the West and
US involvement in Kosovo?

01:56:09.877 --> 01:56:12.460
NOAM CHOMSKY: Kosovo is what's
called a "crisis," unlike, say,

01:56:12.460 --> 01:56:13.550
Angola.

01:56:13.550 --> 01:56:16.770
[LAUGHTER]

01:56:16.770 --> 01:56:17.890
And it's bad.

01:56:17.890 --> 01:56:18.634
It's not a joke.

01:56:18.634 --> 01:56:20.800
Like, there have been a lot
of atrocities in Kosovo.

01:56:20.800 --> 01:56:23.760
Current estimates are that
about 2,000 people were killed.

01:56:23.760 --> 01:56:27.625
It's about a one night
bombing by Jonas Savimbi.

01:56:30.332 --> 01:56:36.070
The reason Kosovo's a crisis
and Angola, which is way worse,

01:56:36.070 --> 01:56:39.970
is not a crisis, is because
Kosovo threatens the interests

01:56:39.970 --> 01:56:42.580
of rich and powerful people.

01:56:42.580 --> 01:56:47.530
I mean, Europe is
disturbed by conflicts

01:56:47.530 --> 01:56:51.190
that are essentially within it.

01:56:51.190 --> 01:56:53.380
And they don't want
them to spread.

01:56:53.380 --> 01:56:55.690
For one thing, there would
be a big flow refugees.

01:56:55.690 --> 01:56:59.170
And for another, if that whole
region starts to fall apart,

01:56:59.170 --> 01:57:02.060
you could end up with wars
between Greece and Turkey

01:57:02.060 --> 01:57:03.970
and maybe a world war.

01:57:03.970 --> 01:57:05.440
Russia might get involved.

01:57:05.440 --> 01:57:06.610
So it matters.

01:57:06.610 --> 01:57:09.620
In Angola, it's just a lot of
those guys killing each other.

01:57:09.620 --> 01:57:10.780
Who cares.

01:57:10.780 --> 01:57:13.240
Besides, it's not a
nice one to talk about,

01:57:13.240 --> 01:57:18.250
since the main killer happens
to be the person who was highly

01:57:18.250 --> 01:57:21.820
praised and lauded as a great
freedom fighter in the United

01:57:21.820 --> 01:57:24.700
States back in the
days of what was called

01:57:24.700 --> 01:57:29.110
"constructive engagement," which
was a nice name for supporting

01:57:29.110 --> 01:57:32.215
South African marauders who
were tearing the place to shreds

01:57:32.215 --> 01:57:35.590
and killing over
a million people.

01:57:35.590 --> 01:57:39.130
Well, one part of it was our
freedom fighter in Angola

01:57:39.130 --> 01:57:40.987
who is now still
tearing the place apart,

01:57:40.987 --> 01:57:42.820
not that the opposition
is so great, either.

01:57:42.820 --> 01:57:44.320
That's another story.

01:57:44.320 --> 01:57:48.970
Anyhow, it's a huge massacre
that doesn't harm Europeans,

01:57:48.970 --> 01:57:52.000
at least as long as
the resources flow out.

01:57:52.000 --> 01:57:54.210
And they are flowing out.

01:57:54.210 --> 01:57:57.400
So then these forces,
UNITA, is being supported

01:57:57.400 --> 01:58:01.240
by mostly diamond flow,
which is monopolized

01:58:01.240 --> 01:58:03.110
by a couple of companies.

01:58:03.110 --> 01:58:04.540
And they can trace--

01:58:04.540 --> 01:58:09.370
technically, there's an embargo
against diamonds from Angola.

01:58:09.370 --> 01:58:11.050
And all the specialists
in the field

01:58:11.050 --> 01:58:13.960
say that it's not hard to
identify the diamonds that

01:58:13.960 --> 01:58:15.380
come from there.

01:58:15.380 --> 01:58:18.485
But what's the name
of that company?

01:58:18.485 --> 01:58:18.984
[SNAPS]

01:58:18.984 --> 01:58:19.900
AUDIENCE: De Beers.

01:58:19.900 --> 01:58:20.540
NOAM CHOMSKY: De Beers, yeah.

01:58:20.540 --> 01:58:21.040
[LAUGHTER]

01:58:21.040 --> 01:58:23.500
The De Beers company
claims they can't do it.

01:58:23.500 --> 01:58:26.020
Only all the other experts
can, but they can't.

01:58:26.020 --> 01:58:29.470
So there's a free flow
of diamonds out of there

01:58:29.470 --> 01:58:30.520
and all sorts of--

01:58:30.520 --> 01:58:32.720
Belgium, Israel,
places like that.

01:58:32.720 --> 01:58:34.420
And that's supporting one side.

01:58:34.420 --> 01:58:35.920
And the other side,
they've got oil,

01:58:35.920 --> 01:58:38.600
which is going right
to Western companies.

01:58:38.600 --> 01:58:40.452
So who cares?

01:58:40.452 --> 01:58:42.160
And meanwhile, they're
killing each other

01:58:42.160 --> 01:58:43.570
or starving or whatever.

01:58:43.570 --> 01:58:46.404
But not bothering rich people.

01:58:46.404 --> 01:58:47.826
So it's not a crisis.

01:58:51.070 --> 01:58:52.570
AUDIENCE: I just
had a [? quick-- ?]

01:58:52.570 --> 01:58:54.964
we've talked about a
lot of things here.

01:58:54.964 --> 01:58:59.682
And a question
was, how do you go

01:58:59.682 --> 01:59:01.140
about keeping track
of these things

01:59:01.140 --> 01:59:03.330
and learning these things
when you acknowledge

01:59:03.330 --> 01:59:04.913
that a lot of them
aren't in the press

01:59:04.913 --> 01:59:07.190
and definitely aren't
in our education?

01:59:07.190 --> 01:59:08.370
How would you suggest
[? that us, ?] [? as ?]

01:59:08.370 --> 01:59:08.550
[? students-- ?]

01:59:08.550 --> 01:59:10.758
NOAM CHOMSKY: Look, I mean,
you can read the journals

01:59:10.758 --> 01:59:14.042
from Penang and Cairo
the same way I do.

01:59:14.042 --> 01:59:16.620
I mean, it takes a lot of work.

01:59:16.620 --> 01:59:19.220
But it can be done.

01:59:19.220 --> 01:59:21.330
And in fact, nowadays,
for those of you

01:59:21.330 --> 01:59:22.380
who do use the internet--

01:59:22.380 --> 01:59:26.190
I don't-- it's a lot easier.

01:59:26.190 --> 01:59:28.830
But the real answer is
nobody can do it alone

01:59:28.830 --> 01:59:30.736
unless they're crazy.

01:59:30.736 --> 01:59:32.310
[LAUGHTER]

01:59:32.910 --> 01:59:34.380
Because you have
to be a fanatic.

01:59:34.380 --> 01:59:36.510
On the other hand, if
people do it in common,

01:59:36.510 --> 01:59:38.530
it's not that hard.

01:59:38.530 --> 01:59:43.560
I mean, you travel through
Midwest churches in the 1980s--

01:59:43.560 --> 01:59:44.460
I mean, I've done it.

01:59:44.460 --> 01:59:49.860
And you come to a church in
Lawrence, Kansas, or someplace,

01:59:49.860 --> 01:59:53.430
they knew more about Central
America than the CIA did.

01:59:53.430 --> 01:59:55.419
Way more.

01:59:55.419 --> 01:59:57.210
Because there was a
lot of people, and they

01:59:57.210 --> 01:59:58.590
cared about it.

01:59:58.590 --> 02:00:00.422
And there were people
who went up and back,

02:00:00.422 --> 02:00:02.130
and they circulated
information, and they

02:00:02.130 --> 02:00:04.350
were in contact with
others, and so on.

02:00:04.350 --> 02:00:06.100
That way, you can
find out about things.

02:00:06.100 --> 02:00:09.090
And in fact, sometimes
remarkably well.

02:00:09.090 --> 02:00:11.760
I mentioned the Multilateral
Agreement on Investments

02:00:11.760 --> 02:00:12.325
a few times.

02:00:12.325 --> 02:00:13.950
If you don't know
that story, you ought

02:00:13.950 --> 02:00:21.720
to, because it's an amazing
story about how activist groups

02:00:21.720 --> 02:00:24.660
around the world, without
any access to the press--

02:00:24.660 --> 02:00:27.900
because the press was keeping
a lid on it for years--

02:00:27.900 --> 02:00:31.160
how much can an
individual scientist do?

02:00:31.160 --> 02:00:33.960
Like, suppose you're
alone in Tahiti,

02:00:33.960 --> 02:00:36.330
and you decide to work
on quantum physics.

02:00:36.330 --> 02:00:38.760
How much are you
going to achieve?

02:00:38.760 --> 02:00:41.940
If you are in a lab at MIT,
where everybody else is

02:00:41.940 --> 02:00:44.310
interested too, and you can
talk to each other and you

02:00:44.310 --> 02:00:46.076
interchange-- and
somebody read something,

02:00:46.076 --> 02:00:47.950
and somebody else comes
in and gives a paper,

02:00:47.950 --> 02:00:49.220
and so on and so forth--

02:00:49.220 --> 02:00:50.640
yeah, you find out
all of a sudden

02:00:50.640 --> 02:00:52.800
it's magnified quite a lot.

02:00:52.800 --> 02:00:54.660
It's not different in this case.

02:00:54.660 --> 02:00:57.180
That's part of the reason
for the enormous efforts that

02:00:57.180 --> 02:00:59.760
are made to separate
people from one another.

02:00:59.760 --> 02:01:03.720
I mean, that's why there's a
barrage literally from infancy

02:01:03.720 --> 02:01:06.750
to separate people
from one another.

02:01:06.750 --> 02:01:09.570
And it's conscious--
quite self-conscious.

02:01:09.570 --> 02:01:13.050
You read the literature of
the public relations industry.

02:01:13.050 --> 02:01:14.030
They explain it.

02:01:14.030 --> 02:01:15.810
And it, in fact, goes way back.

02:01:15.810 --> 02:01:18.630
If people get together,
they're dangerous.

02:01:18.630 --> 02:01:20.380
You keep them isolated
from one another,

02:01:20.380 --> 02:01:21.650
there isn't a lot they can do.

02:01:21.650 --> 02:01:24.620
Oh, you can be
angry about things.

02:01:24.620 --> 02:01:27.120
And that's really the answer.

02:01:27.120 --> 02:01:29.700
JANE GOULD: Clearly
our turnout tonight

02:01:29.700 --> 02:01:34.170
suggests that these are
issues of huge importance

02:01:34.170 --> 02:01:37.920
to this community, that your
voice, Noam Chomsky, is one

02:01:37.920 --> 02:01:40.170
we want and need to hear.

02:01:40.170 --> 02:01:42.690
We could go with
questions forever.

02:01:42.690 --> 02:01:43.890
But we mustn't.

02:01:43.890 --> 02:01:44.700
Thank you, Noam.

02:01:44.700 --> 02:01:45.533
Thank you, audience.

02:01:45.533 --> 02:01:48.650
[APPLAUSE]

